{B} = \\{{1}, {t}, {t^2} \\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125769 1064 "A set of vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis called a **basis** of an abstract vector space $V$ if the following two conditions hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nFor example, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ {\\color{blue}{1}}, {\\color{blue}{t}}, {\\color{blue}{t^2}} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIs $\\mathcal{B}$ a basis for $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]?$ Let's check our two conditions.\n\n- First, we check for linear independence. Notice that the equation \n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} + x_2 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t} + x_3 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\n \nis true for all $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\nx_1=x_2=x_3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\n- Next, we check that $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$ Notice that we can write any polynomial $at^2+bt+c\\in\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ as a linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nat^2+bt+c=c\\cdot{\\color{blue}1} + b\\cdot{\\color{blue}t} + a\\cdot{\\color{blue}t^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {1}, {t}, {t^2} \\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125770 1064 "A set of vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of a vector space $V$ if $\\mathcal{B}$ is a **maximally linearly independent set in $V$**. In other words, $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent and for any vector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\in V$, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} \\cup \\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\} = \\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\}\n$$\n\nis linearly dependent." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125770 1064 "A set of vectors $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ is a basis of a vector space $V$ if $\\mathcal{B}$ is a **maximally linearly independent set in $V$**. In other words, $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent and for any vector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\in V,$ the set \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} \\cup \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\} = \\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\}\n$$\n\nis linearly dependent." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125771 1064 "Let $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ denote the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ that have degree at most $n$. Also, let $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ be the vector space of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$. Then, we have the following important standard bases.\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ is $\\{1, \\: t \\}$.\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is $\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2 \\}$.\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is $\\{1, \\: t, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n \\}$.\n- The standard basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125771 1064 "Let $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ denote the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ that have degree at most $n.$ Also, let $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ be the vector space of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$ Then, we have the following important standard bases.\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2 \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125784 1064 "We already learned that the rank of a matrix $A$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. Now, can we obtain any information from the *non-pivot* columns?\n\nThe answer is yes! The non-pivot columns help to find the dimension of the null space of a matrix $A$, and here's how.\n\nConsider the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix $A$ is already in row echelon form, where the $1$st and the $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd one is a *non-pivot* column. Now, to get the null space of $A$, we need to find the solution of the system\n\n$$\n{3x_{1} + 5x_{2} + x_{3} = 0; 2x_{3} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and ${\\color{blue}x_3}$ are basic variables and $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable. Hence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ and, therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))={\\color{red}1}\n$$\n\n.\n\nIn other words,\n\n> $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is equal to the number of *non-pivot* columns in the corresponding row echelon form of $A$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n> $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is the number of *free variables* in the corresponding homogeneous system with the coefficient matrix $A$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125784 1064 "We already learned that the rank of a matrix $A$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ Now, can we obtain any information from the *non-pivot* columns?\n\nThe answer is yes! The non-pivot columns help to find the dimension of the null space of a matrix $A,$ and here's how.\n\nConsider the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ is already in row echelon form, where the $1$st and the $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd one is a *non-pivot* column. Now, to get the null space of $A,$ we need to find the solution of the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and ${\\color{blue}x_3}$ are basic variables and $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable. Hence, we obtain that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))={\\color{red}1}$.\n\nIn other words,\n\n> $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is equal to the number of *non-pivot* columns in the corresponding row echelon form of $A$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n> $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$  is the number of *free variables* in the corresponding homogeneous system with the coefficient matrix $A.$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125785 1064 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix $A$ is often called the **nullity** of $A$. We will also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A))\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125785 1064 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix $A$ is often called the **nullity** of $A$. We will also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)).\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125786 1064 "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A$. Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas only the trivial solution\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that $\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$, or alternatively, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0$.\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n$.\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n> *Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n> $Col(A) = R^{n}; dim(Col(A)) = n = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125786 1064 "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n> *Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n> $$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125791 1064 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices.\n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$, we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}λ & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & λ\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 - λ & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 - λ\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 - λ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-2 - λ)(-8 - λ)(5 - λ) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)(λ + 8)(λ - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2$, $\\lambda=-8$, and $\\lambda=5$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125791 1064 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125792 1064 "The **algebraic multiplicity** of an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is the number of times it is a solution of the characteristic equation.\n\nFor example, consider a $6\\times 6$ matrix $A$ with the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda+8)^{\\color{blue}3} =0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda={\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nis a double root. Therefore, it has algebraic multiplicity $2$.\n\nBy the same reasoning, we say that\n\n- $\\lambda=1$ has algebraic multiplicity $1$, and\n- $\\lambda=-8$ has algebraic multiplicity $\\color{blue}3$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125792 1064 "The **algebraic multiplicity** of an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is the number of times it is a solution of the characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, consider a $6\\times 6$ matrix $A$ with the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda+8)^{\\color{blue}3} =0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{red}4}$ is a double root. Therefore, it has algebraic multiplicity $2.$ \n\nBy the same reasoning, we say that \n\n- $\\lambda=1$ has algebraic multiplicity $1,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=-8$ has algebraic multiplicity $\\color{blue}3$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125793 1064 "Let's consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwith the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$. Note that this eigenvalue has multiplicity two in the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWe know how to find eigenvectors when all eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic equation (i.e., all roots have multiplicity equal to one). But what can we say about the eigenspace $V_{-3}$ for this particular matrix?\n\nRecall that given a matrix with eigenvalue $\\lambda$, the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ is\n\n$$\neigenvectors\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of the matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -11\\end{bmatrix} - (-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{8x_{1} + 8x_{2} + 16x_{3} = 0; 4x_{1} + 4x_{2} + 8x_{3} = 0; - 4x_{1} - 4x_{2} - 8x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSolving the system, we obtain the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} - 2x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our eigenspace is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that $s=t=0$ gives the zero vector. On the other hand, the vectors from the general solution form a basis $\\mathcal B$ of our eigenspace:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn particular, we have\n\n$$\n\\dim(V_{-3}) =2\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125793 1064 "Let's consider the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwith the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3.$ Note that this eigenvalue has multiplicity two in the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWe know how to find eigenvectors when all eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic equation (i.e., all roots have multiplicity equal to one). But what can we say about the eigenspace $V_{-3}$ for this particular matrix?\n\nRecall that given a matrix with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{eigenvectors}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of the matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -11\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 