 $\\mathbf{T}({\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_3})$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{B \\mid C} &= [ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\\\\n&= [T(b_{1})]_{C} \\mid [T(b_{2})]_{C} \\mid [T(b_{3})]_{C} \\\\\n&= \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T]_{B \\mid C} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 10 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125813 1064 "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0)$. If $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0) = 0\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186125813 1064 "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0).$   If  $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P.$ The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0)   = 0.\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186125822 1064 "So, given a stationary critical point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R},$ how do we classify it?\n\nOne way is to consider the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $\\mathbf{a}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\