 and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) > 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) > f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *minimum*.\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, this quadratic form is always negative, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) < 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) < f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *maximum.*\n\n- If this quadratic form is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point.\n\nThe assumption $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0$ is important. If $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) =0,$ then the quadratic form is zero along some line through $\\mathbf a$ or zero *everywhere* in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a.$ In such cases, we cannot ignore the higher-order terms and must use another method to classify the critical point.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider cases where $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0.$\n\nFor example, suppose we're told that [math]\\mathbf{a}[/math] is a critical point of the function [math]f[/math] and\n\n\\[\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) =\n\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThen, we can classify the critical point by classifying [math]\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})[/math] as the matrix of a quadratic form.\n\nCalculating the eigenvalues of the matrix, we find that one is positive and the other is negative:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\lambda_1 &= -2 < 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\\lambda_2 &= 3 > 0\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. T