t process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1.$ This can be achieved by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}.$\nMoreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\},$ which is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186135060 1064 "The **Gram-Schmidt process** is an algorithm that we can use to construct an *orthogonal* basis if we are given a non-orthogonal basis to start with.\n\nFor example, consider the basis $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ for the subspace\n\n$$\nV=\\textrm{Span} \\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can use the above basis to construct an *orthogonal* basis for the given $2$-dimensional subspace. To do this, we use the Gram-Schmidt process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1$.\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1$. This can be achieved by computing\n$v_{2}|= a_{2}- proj_{v_{1}}a_{2}; = a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1}$.![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$. So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V$, and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}$.\nMoreover,\n$\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}$.\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\}\n$$\n\nwhich is an orthogonal basis for $V$.\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{2} &= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} \\\\\n&= a_{2} - (4 \\cdot 1 + 6 \\cdot (-1))/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2})v_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} - (-1) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186135061 1064 "Given a basis $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ for a subspace $V$, we can use the Gram-Schmidt process to find an *orthonormal* basis of the subspace.\n\nTo do this, we simply execute the Gram-Schmidt process as before to obtain an orthogonal basis $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\}$, and then we normalize each vector in this basis to get the orthonormal basis $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2\\}$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_1 \\Vert}, \\quad \\mathbf{u}_2 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_2 \\Vert}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186135061 1064 "Given a basis $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ for a subspace $V,$ we can use the Gram-Schmidt process to find an *orthonormal* basis of the subspace.\n\nTo do this, we simply execute the Gram-Schmidt process as before to obtain an orthogonal basis $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\},$ and then we normalize each vector in this basis to get the orthonormal basis $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2\\},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_1 \\Vert},\n\t\\quad\n\t\\mathbf{u}_2 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_2 \\Vert}.\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186135095 1064 "Sometimes it's helpful to \"clean up\" the result of a Gram-Schmidt process by scaling the resulting vectors.\n\nFor example, suppose that the set $\\mathcal B$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$.\n\nSince the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$, the vector $3\\mathbf{v}_2$ also lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp 3\\mathbf{v}_2$. Therefore, taking\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ninstead of $\\mathbf{v}_2$, we again obtain an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBut this time, the second vector in the basis looks slightly nicer and is probably easier to work with." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186135095 1064 "Sometimes it's helpful to \"clean up\" the result of a Gram-Schmidt process by scaling the resulting vectors.\n\nFor example, suppose that the set $\\mathcal B,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}.$\n\nSince the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2,$ the vector $3\\mathbf{v}_2$ also lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp 3\\mathbf{v}_2.$ Therefore, taking \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ninstead of $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we again obtain an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBut this time, the second vector in the basis looks slightly nicer and is probably easier to work with." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186135096 1064 "A set of vectors\n\n$$\n{U}=\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that $\\mathbf u_i$ is a unit vector if\n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf u_i \\Vert = 1\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u_i \\cdot \\mathbf u_i = 1\n$$\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{e}_1, \\mathbf{e}_2, \\mathbf{e}_3\\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n- An orthogonal set in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors. More precisely, if $V = \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\dots, \\mathbf{v}_p\\}$ is an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors and $\\mathbf{v}_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$, then we must have that $p\\leq n$.\n\n**Note:** To remember that orthogonal set in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors, you can use the following intuitive mnemonic. There are only $n$ dimensions in $\\mathbb{R}^n$, and each orthogonal vector has a $1$-dimensional span, meaning that it \"takes up\" $1$ dimension of $\\mathbb{R}^n$. So, you can't \"fit\" more than $n$ orthogonal vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186135096 1064 "A set of vectors ![](images/96/969c7c332d8285fa0419e716cad301755b3ff1f9f074ab55798ef7c6ea09b8a1.svg) is an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that ![](images/25/257a1924bd39970f89e8a9becbac5a5f260572a7e181dedc91ebcc4bb04b0362.svg) is a unit vector if ![](images/e5/e5ae26b69002c7beb7ee09911c2f5da8f62cd1c4ab687657a7e995d89c3f319a.svg) or equivalently, ![](images/d8/d8b5f1f6c4f3c8955cffeac4e7a4407b0d7a5e26afbf95d87e6dd71cc83c0c47.svg)\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis ![](images/f8/f80114a44b31082d68abe17a7d45f60a6455a147a82001ef548eaed69c0c609d.svg) of ![](images/46/4607eb52aa0195587414ef27616970a6b47226a3bb476e944ac079422dc1e1b0.svg) is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n\n- An orthogonal set in ![](images/5b/5b243567f6e2c78e286689189eb1336948b7e67d92e7c15472ca77