476 1064 "Given an orthogonal basis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n\\}\n$$\n\nof\n\n$$\nS=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathcal{B}\\}\n$$\n\nthere is an easy way to find the components of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in S$ with respect to this basis. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFurthermore, if the basis is *orthonormal*, then we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{q}_i\\cdot\\mathbf{q}_i=1\n$$\n\n. So, the formula for each component is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186158476 1064 "Given an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n\\}$ of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathcal{B}\\},$ there is an easy way to find the components of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in S$ with respect to this basis. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFurthermore, if the basis is *orthonormal*, then we know that $\\mathbf{q}_i\\cdot\\mathbf{q}_i=1$. So, the formula for each component is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186158477 1064 "Given any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and any subspace $W$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$, we can always write $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of a vector from $W$ and a vector from $W^{\\perp}$. This fact is known as the **orthogonal decomposition theorem.** We can state it formally as follows:\n\n> *Let $W$ be a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$. Then each $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ can be written uniquely in the form\n> $\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z}$\n> where $\\mathbf{y} \\in W$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\in W^{\\perp}$.*\n\n> *Moreover, since $\\mathbf y$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf x$ onto the subspace $W$, if $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1\\,\\dots,\\mathbf{u}_p\\}$ is any orthogonal basis of $W$, then*\n> $y|= proj_{W}x; = proj_{u_{1}}x + proj_{u_{2}}x + ⋯ + proj_{u_{p}}x; = \\frac{x \\cdot u_{1}}{u_{1} \\cdot u_{1}}u_{1} + \\frac{x \\cdot u_{2}}{u_{2} \\cdot u_{2}}u_{2} + ⋯ + \\frac{x \\cdot u_{p}}{u_{p} \\cdot u_{p}}u_{p}$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186158477 1064 "Given any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and any subspace $W$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we can always write $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of a vector from $W$ and a vector from $W^{\\perp}.$ This fact is known as the **orthogonal decomposition theorem.** We can state it formally as follows:\n\n> *Let $W$ be a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then each $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ can be written uniquely in the form\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> where $\\mathbf{y} \\in W$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\in W^{\\perp}.$*\n\n> *Moreover, since $\\mathbf y$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf x$ onto the subspace $W,$ if $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1\\,\\dots,\\mathbf{u}_p\\}$ is any orthogonal basis of $W,$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{W}\\,x \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{1}}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{2}}\\,x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{p}}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{1}}{{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}}{u}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{2}}{{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}}{u}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{p}}{{u}_{p}\\cdot {u}_{p}}{u}_{p}.\\end{aligned}\n> $$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186158498 1064 "A **geometric distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nIf $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of experiments needed before the first success has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p, \\qquad x=1,2,3,\\ldots\n$$\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a geometric distribution with probability $p$, then we write $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(p)$.\n\nFor example, suppose we repeatedly flip a coin until we get heads. Each flip of the coin is a binomial experiment, and the probability of success is\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of flips needed in order to get heads. Then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - p)^{x - 1}p \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{1}{2})^{x - 1}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{x - 1}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the probability of needing $3$ tosses until we get heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= f(3) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\\\\n&= 0.125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186158498 1064 "A **geometric distribution** is a probability distribution that models the total number of independent Bernoulli trials until the first success occurs.\n\nIf ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of experiments needed before the first success has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/c6/c64756e38cf4152fdbcd3c3cb3c99e6368b7ec2549f6ea96e6badedeb7607cd8.svg)\n\nIf a random variable ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) follows a geometric distribution with probability ![](images/f4/f43942df7283a860efbcb722d0d28b46eca1b4d2ffc58a91a1858e36b0947101.svg) then we write ![](images/d1/d196f25e129c7b7bb4bdfff7f0383f5fa79d352b82ca3a0d8634fe7a64e0dc8c.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose we repeatedly flip a coin until we get heads. Each flip of the coin is a binomial experiment, and the probability of success is ![](images/f9/f98838fb4a565f05ea528dffcab8dad9e199e52bb3a2dfe071d6e11955e9ceb6.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) be the number of flips needed in order to get heads. Then ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/21/21bb1ed33c23f67b65a0c2337d7a34c17cd3e4f663b0b95737fff31bbafdf055.svg)\n\nIn particular, the probability of needing ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) tosses until we get heads is \n\n![](images/3c/3c965ff9951df068207847eb1c5eb105d486195f44f79adb654e43c8e50690c2.svg)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186158500 1064 "The shape of the graph of the geometric distribution depends on the value of $p$. When $p$ is large, the probabilities are concentrated at small values of $x$. When $p$ is smaller, the probabilities are more spread out.\n\n![](images/51/51c0438105701a9ef6a19c280dde7bdddf6ccb82bd1ab487d2fdaf902bcca534.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186158500 1064 "The shape of the graph of the geometric distribution depends on the value of $p.$ When $p$ is large, the probabilities are concentrated at small values of $x.$ When $p$ is smaller, the probabilities are more spread out.\n\n![](images/51/51c0438105701a9ef6a19c280dde7bdddf6ccb82bd1ab487d2fdaf902bcca534.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186158574 1064 "We've seen how to calculate the total derivative of a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$. In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate its second derivative.\n\nFor a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe total derivative of $f$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate $f$ once more, we first need to take the transpose of the first derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the **second derivative** of $f$, we simply apply the definition of the total derivative to\n\n$$\n\\left(\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)\\right)^T\n$$\n\nBy doing this, we get the following $n\\times n$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that this matrix is *symmetric* due to the equality of mixed partial derivatives. This becomes important later.\n\nThe matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x)$ is sometimes called the **Hessian matrix,** and its determinant,\n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det\\big(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\big)\n$$\n\nis called the **Hessian determinant**.\n\nNote that in the case of a two-variable function $f(x,y)$, the second derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll primarily focus on two-variable functions $f(x,y)$. However, we should remember that the techniques discussed here can be applied to scalar functions with an arbitrary number of variables." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186158574 1064 "We've seen how to calculate the total derivative of a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R.$ In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate its second derivative.\n\nFor a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe total derivative of $f$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo differentiate $f$ once more, we first need to take the transpose of the first derivative:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the **second derivative** of $f,$ we simply apply the definition of the total derivative to $\\left(\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)\\right)^T.$ By doing this, we get the following $n\\times n$ matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that this matrix is *symmetric* due to the equality of mixed partial derivatives. This becomes important later. \n\nThe matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x)$ is sometimes called the **Hessian matrix,** and its determinant, \n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det\\big( \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\big)\n$$\n\nis called the **Hessian determinant**.\n\nNote that in the case of a two-variable function $f(x,y),$ the second derivative is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll primarily focus on two-variable functions $f(x,y).$ However, we should remember that the techniques discussed here can be applied to scalar functions with an arbitrary number of variables." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163233 1064 "Suppose we are given the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\nand the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ that defines the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nHow do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}$?\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= 2y_{1} - y_{2} \\\\\nx_{2} &= -y_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} - 8x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (2y_{1} - y_{2})^{2} + 8(2y_{1} - y_{2})y_{2} + (-y_{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 4y_{1}^{2} - 4y_{1}y_{2} + y_{2}^{2} + 16y_{1}y_{2} - 8y_{2}^{2} + y_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 4y_{1}^{2} + 12y_{1}y_{2} - 6y_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163233 1064 "Suppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\n and the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ that defines  the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n How do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}?$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+8(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+16{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163236 1064 "In certain applications involving quadratic forms, an important question is whether a particular quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$ is *always* positive or *always* negative.\n\nWe classify quadratic forms using the following terminology:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) > 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form\n\n$$\nS(\\mathbf{x})=8x_1^2+3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis positive-definite since\n$8x_1^2+3x_2^2 > 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/52/52e43ebb6fa34f40a73358f88aed62846332126c4fe080072c211945f54b412a.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) <     0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form \n\n$$\nT(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2-3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis negative-definite since\n$-x_1^2-3x_2^2 \\lt 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86f80966c04f960823db91bfc8711eef2bf22a81502e69b5048afc5b4799cb2.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **indefinite** if \n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_1) > 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_2) <     0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form\n\n$$\nR(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+2x_2^2-6x_1x_2\n$$\n\nis indefinite. Substituting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0-0 \\\\  & =1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+2-6 \\\\  & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163236 1064 "In certain applications involving quadratic forms, an important question is whether a particular quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$ is *always* positive or *always* negative.\n\nWe classify quadratic forms using the following terminology:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) > 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.For example, the quadratic form\n$S(\\mathbf{x})=8x_1^2+3x_2^2$\nis positive-definite since\n$8x_1^2+3x_2^2 > 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}$.![](images/52/52e43ebb6fa34f40a73358f88aed62846332126c4fe080072c211945f54b412a.png)\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) < 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.For example, the quadratic form\n$T(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2-3x_2^2$\nis negative-definite since\n$-x_1^2-3x_2^2 \\lt 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}$.![](images/a8/a86f80966c04f960823db91bfc8711eef2bf22a81502e69b5048afc5b4799cb2.png)\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **indefinite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}_1) > 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and$Q(\\mathbf{x}_2) < 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$.For example, the form\n$R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+2x_2^2-6x_1x_2$\nis indefinite. Substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n$R(x_{1})|= 1^{2} + 2 \\cdot 0^{2} - 6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0; = 1 + 0 - 0; = 1 > 0$,\nwhile substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we obtain\n$R(x_{2})|= 1^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1^{2} - 6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1; = 1 + 2 - 6; =-3 < 0$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163242 1064 "Suppose we are given a quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x^{T}Ax \\\\\n&= (Py)^{T}A(Py) \\\\\n&= y^{T}P^{T}APy \\\\\n&= y^{T}(P^{T}AP)y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new matrix of our quadratic form is\n\n$$\nB= P^T \\! A P\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163242 1064 "Suppose we are given a quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines  the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}{P}^{T}\\,AP\\,y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}AP)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the new matrix of our quadratic form is \n\n$$\nB=  P^T \\! A P.\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163243 1064 "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n> *The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* \n> \n> $$\n> B = P^T \\! A \\, P.\n> $$\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from  $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R.$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163243 1064 "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n> *The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* $B = P^T \\! A \\, P$.\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R$." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163247 1064 "Given a quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwith a symmetric matrix $A$, we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative,\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive,\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nUsing the same criteria, we can also classify the symmetric matrix $A$ as positive-definite, positive semi-definite, etc.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nComputing the characteristic equation of $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-5 - λ & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-5 - λ)(-5 - λ) - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 10λ + 24 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 4)(λ + 6) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= -4,-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\, \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n(as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163247 1064 "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with a symmetric matrix $A$,  we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive,\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nUsing the same criteria, we can also classify the symmetric matrix $A$ as positive-definite, positive semi-definite, etc.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nComputing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +6) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4,\\,-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) =  \\mathbf{x}^T A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163248 1064 "Let ![](images/c6/c6f4a56eed9073b3541e4398db9fc99f2e8ca94f651762e1f650bfe54865ca49.svg) for ![](images/16/1621ad9f5426c8fe19e2d5a00c5f92a60420aeaed7bfe623db70c9ab4e57527d.svg)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) \n\nAccording to **Sylvester's criterion**, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg) we have ![](images/03/039aff55ff839029e398761cdb94f09b8c2203413637d6f41ca9c680659cc8d2.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is odd, and ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is even.\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is negative-definite, then the leading principal minors alternate in sign, with the *first* leading principal minors being negative.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n\n![](images/bc/bc72eb8a57a1af3a4cc1526c071f7611131359d87b1ed56417e626aad662c9cb.svg)\n\nLet's compute its leading leading principal minors:\n\n![](images/84/844bf128565ccaeee211fcbeca2179b870901879303ce3e911956d65b468926f.svg)\n\nAll leading principal minors are positive. Therefore, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) is positive-definite." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163248 1064 "Let $\\Delta_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\ndenote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A$.\n\nAccording to **Sylvester's criterion**, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is\n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$,\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nIn particular, if $A$ is negative-definite, then the principal minors alternate in sign, with the *first* principal minor being negative.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's compute its principal minors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔ_{1} &= a_{11} = 2 > 0 \\\\\nΔ_{2} &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1|= 1 > 0 \\\\ Δ_{3}|= & A\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 5 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll principal minors are positive. Therefore, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is positive-definite." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163324 1064 "There are two further classifications of quadratic forms that turn out to be useful:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$.For example, the form $A(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is positive semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not positive-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$![](images/8d/8d0adf62e90acc2f5a2a3fdbad2b87fc53e4f50145e38d8120cd932282afbb42.png)\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$. For example, the form $B(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is negative semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not negative-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$![](images/76/76feacaad709f2f3a3c1dac8c0ee88aa461aed827270d0127f61de38d95489b6.png)\n\nIt's also worth noting that\n\n- any positive-definite quadratic form is positive semi-definite, and\n- any negative-definite quadratic form is negative semi-definite." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163324 1064 "There are two further classifications of quadratic forms that turn out to be useful:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form $A(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is positive semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not positive-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0adf62e90acc2f5a2a3fdbad2b87fc53e4f50145e38d8120cd932282afbb42.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ \n\nFor example, the form $B(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is negative semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not negative-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n![](images/76/76feacaad709f2f3a3c1dac8c0ee88aa461aed827270d0127f61de38d95489b6.png)\n\nIt's also worth noting that\n\n- any positive-definite quadratic form is positive semi-definite, and\n\n- any negative-definite quadratic form is negative semi-definite." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163325 1064 "The **leading principal minors** of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix are the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) determinants formed by the first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) rows and first ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) columns of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) for ![](images/02/025dfd41d08c5cd679c6635316e6d92c7b612c1db8a0d5c88ca3633eb5dc3d0d.svg) The leading principal minors of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) are denoted ![](images/d4/d4d4f854b6186e5a619b024a48f4017756b1de3fcf8f3c90d80e5a251bc3de93.svg)\n\nNote that:\n\n- the first leading principal minors ![](images/e9/e983ef296ac0b20ed0beb7771d7616ab810886d775bd8e343f03f78c757ee678.svg) equals ![](images/1c/1caa82c7c23331cc78782a151b62bafd5f6d9acee12c7ae29dc74556ebc16c1c.svg) and\n\n- the ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg)th leading principal minors ![](images/a2/a27bb42bcd37b2e4ba1416cff413d166b960a686a26ca5085da9be8661d8faf8.svg) equals ![](images/3a/3a950a6005df11dc3df83b40a796c8e5ce962b591e040207bb961da532ec92dd.svg)\n\nFor example, consider the matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by\n\n![](images/26/261bd51313b7630f2c14550d0318e1094a7915316c165e8555ddd77148b8b26d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is a ![](images/59/599d5897394ff1110f6a3bac7053c6836d231de17effad76a1c633d1aed81b33.svg) matrix, it has ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) leading principal minors. Let's compute them:\n\n- The first leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first row and first column of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n![](images/9a/9ab81179136bdf16c09d64def927c7175884692ecd6f359143bb7333e91b3a2b.svg)\n\n- The second leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) rows and first ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n![](images/03/032d2edce1d92da3a0b49aa2e582f61896280571c9fd2c1cc277b7cc77af353e.svg)\n\n- The third leading principal minors is the determinant formed by the first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) rows and first ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) columns of ![](images/18/18e24ea087f70c1cc4a9689aa1571074db08e3e830283838c983a5c4fb976602.svg)\n\n![](images/9d/9d1f53a40f9e6dfcb44f231287b67b70a42d1cd10dbf99c803517f21af7265eb.svg)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163325 1064 "The **principal minors** of an $n\\times n$ matrix are the $n$ determinants formed by the first $r$ rows and first $r$ columns of $A$ for\n\n$$\nr = 1,2,3,\\ldots, n\n$$\n\nThe principal minors of $A$ are denoted $\\Delta_i$.\n\nNote that:\n\n- the first principal minor $\\Delta_1$ equals $a_{11}$, and\n- the $n$th principal minor $\\Delta_n$ equals $\\det(A)$.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is a $3\\times3$ matrix, it has $3$ principal minors. Let's compute them:\n\n- The first principal minor is the determinant formed by the first row and first column of $A$:\n$\\mid a_{11} \\mid$\n- The second principal minor is the determinant formed by the first $2$ rows and first $2$ columns of $A$:\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}$\n- The third principal minor is the determinant formed by the first $3$ rows and first $3$ columns of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186163325 1067 "The Leading Principal Minors of a Square Matrix" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186163325 1067 "The Principal Minors of a Square Matrix" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186167983 1064 "Given two solutions to a linear system of differential equations, any *constant-coefficient* linear combination of those solutions is also a solution to the system. This property of linear systems is called the **linearity principle** (or the **superposition principle**).\n\nLet's state this again, more precisely. If $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to the homogeneous matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthen the expression\n$c \\, \\mathbf{u}(t) + d \\, \\mathbf{v}(t)$\nis also a solution to that equation for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nWe can prove this by substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x(t) = c\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\ninto the matrix differential equation and verifying that it results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(t) &= Ax(t) \\\\\n(cu(t) + dv(t))^{′} &= A(cu(t) + dv(t)) \\\\\ncu^{′}(t) + dv^{′}(t) &= cAu(t) + dAv(t) \\\\\ncu^{′}(t) + dv^{′}(t) &= cu^{′}(t) + dv^{′}(t)✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c\\,\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\,\\mathbf{v}(t)$ is a solution!\n\nIn general, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations states that if\n$\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t), \\: \\ldots, \\: \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$\nare solutions to a homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthen any linear combination\n$c_1 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k \\, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system.\n\nNext, let's see a concrete example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186167983 1064 "Given two solutions to a linear system of differential equations, any *constant-coefficient* linear combination of those solutions is also a solution to the system. This property of linear systems is called the **linearity principle** (or the **superposition principle**).\n\nLet's state this again, more precisely. If $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to the homogeneous matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \nthen the expression \n\n$$\nc \\, \\mathbf{u}(t) + d \\, \\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n \nis also a solution to that equation for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWe can prove this by substituting $\\mathbf x(t) = c\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\mathbf{v}(t)$ into the matrix differential equation and verifying that it results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ (c\\,u(t)+d\\,v(t))^{'} & =A(c\\,u(t)+d\\,v(t)) \\\\ c\\,{u}^{'}(t)+d\\,{v}^{'}(t) & =cA\\,u(t)+dA\\,v(t) \\\\ c\\,{u}^{'}(t)+d\\,{v}^{'}(t) & =c\\,{u}^{'}(t)+d\\,{v}^{'}(t)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c\\,\\mathbf{u}(t) +d\\,\\mathbf{v}(t)$ is a solution!\n\nIn general, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations states that if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t), \\: \\ldots, \\: \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nare solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2 \\, \\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k \\, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system.\n\nNext, let's see a concrete example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186167984 1064 "If $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a *homogeneous* linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an *inhomogeneous* linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is some nonzero vector of functions, then\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\nis also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nThis gives us the **general solution** structure:\n\n$$\n\\text{(every solution)} = \\text{(homogeneous solution)} + \\text{(a particular solution)}\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186167984 1064 "If $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a *homogeneous* linear system \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an *inhomogeneous* linear system \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is some nonzero vector of functions, then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nThis gives us the **general solution** structure:\n\n$$\n\\text{(every solution)} = \\text{(homogeneous solution)} + \\text{(a particular solution)}\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186167985 1064 "A **decoupled system** of ODEs is a system in which each equation involves only its own dependent variable. For a linear homogeneous system, this means each equation has the form\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.\n\nFor example, the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 5x_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis decoupled, while the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled.\n\nIn matrix form, a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nis decoupled exactly when $A$ is a diagonal matrix. For example:\n\n- The system\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nis decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n- The system\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nis *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186167985 1064 "A **decoupled system** of ODEs is a system in which each equation involves only its own dependent variable. For a linear homogeneous system, this means each equation has the form\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nFor example, the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis decoupled, while the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled.\n\nIn matrix form, a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nis decoupled exactly when $A$ is a diagonal matrix. For example:\n\n- The system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- The system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186167986 1064 "Now, let's consider the following decoupled system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is diagonal, we can solve the two differential equations separately using elementary calculus:\n\n- The solution to $x_1'(t)={3}x_1(t)$ is $x_1(t)=c_1e^{{3}t}$ for $c_1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- The solution to $x_2'(t)={-5}x_2(t)$ is $x_2(t)=c_2e^{{-5}t}$ for $c_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the solution of the system as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn general, for a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe formula for the **general solution** is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the set \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nalways forms a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions to the system." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186167986 1064 "Now, let's consider the following decoupled system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = 3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-5x_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is diagonal, we can solve the two differential equations separately using elementary calculus:\n\n- The solution to $x_1'(t)={3}x_1(t)$ is $x_1(t)=c_1e^{{3}t}$ for $c_1\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n- The solution to $x_2'(t)={-5}x_2(t)$ is $x_2(t)=c_2e^{{-5}t}$ for $c_2\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore, we can write the solution of the system as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn general, for a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = λ_{1}x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = λ_{2}x_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nthe formula for the **general solution** is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNote that the set\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nalways forms a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions to the system." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186167987 1064 "Consider the following decoupled system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the *general solution* to this is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBut sometimes we may need to find a **particular solution** where we give specific values to the constants $c_1$ and $c_2.$\n\nFor example, suppose that $x_1$ and $x_2$ represent the number of birds of species $A$ and $B,$ respectively, that inhabit a particular island, and the above system models the growth rates of the two species. \n\nGiven this information, we might want to know how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years. To answer this question, we need an **initial condition**, which is some extra information about the system at a particular moment in time. \n\nSuppose that at $t=0,$ there were initially $20$ birds of species $A$ and $2\\,000$ birds of species $B.$ We can write this initial condition as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(0) \\\\ {x}_{2}(0)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, by substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we can find specific values of $c_1$ and $c_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c_1 = 20$ and $c_2 = 2\\,000$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years by substituting $t=3$ into the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =20[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3}+2\\,000[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\approx [\\begin{aligned}401.7 \\\\ 99.6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that there will be approximately $402$ birds of species $A$ and $100$ birds of species $B$ after $t=3$ years." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186167987 1064 "Consider the following decoupled system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nWe already know that the *general solution* to this is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBut sometimes we may need to find a **particular solution** where we give specific values to the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$.\n\nFor example, suppose that $x_1$ and $x_2$ represent the number of birds of species $A$ and $B$, respectively, that inhabit a particular island, and the above system models the growth rates of the two species.\n\nGiven this information, we might want to know how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years. To answer this question, we need an **initial condition**, which is some extra information about the system at a particular moment in time.\n\nSuppose that at $t=0$, there were initially $20$ birds of species $A$ and $2\\,000$ birds of species $B$. We can write this initial condition as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(0) \\\\ x_{2}(0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, by substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we can find specific values of $c_1$ and $c_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= \\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 2000\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nc_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 2000\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 2000\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c_1 = 20$ and\n\n$$\nc_2 = 2\\,000\n$$\n\n.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years by substituting $t=3$ into the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(3) &= 20\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{3} + 2000\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}20e^{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2000e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}20e^{3} \\\\ 2000e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}401.7 \\\\ 99.6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that there will be approximately $402$ birds of species $A$ and $100$ birds of species $B$ after $t=3$ years." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186167996 1064 "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={a}_{11}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={a}_{21}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t},$ $t^2,$ or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+8{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+10{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 8+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186167996 1064 "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = a_{11}x_{1}(t) + a_{12}x_{2}(t) + f_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = a_{21}x_{1}(t) + a_{22}x_{2}(t) + f_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t}$, $t^2$, or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = 4x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t) + 8e^{3t}; x_{2}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + 5x_{2}(t) + 10e^{4t}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′}(t) = 4 \\cdot x_{1}(t) + 2 \\cdot x_{2}(t) + e^{3t} \\cdot 8 + e^{4t} \\cdot 0; x_{2}^{′}(t) = (-3) \\cdot x_{1}(t) + 5 \\cdot x_{2}(t) + e^{3t} \\cdot 0 + e^{4t} \\cdot 10\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}(t) \\\\ x_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168000 1064 "Consider a system of two differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\boldsymbol{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwhere $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}(x(t),y(t)) \\\\ {f}_{2}(x(t),y(t))\\end{aligned}]$ is a vector-valued function of $x(t)$ and $y(t).$\n\nIn this context, the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the **phase plane** (or **phase space**) of the system.\nAt each time $t,$ the solution has a position\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich we view as a point $(x(t),y(t))$ in the phase plane. Now, as $t$ changes, the point $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ moves and traces out a curve in the phase plane.\nThis curve is called the **trajectory** (or **orbit**) of the solution.\n\nFor instance, consider the following (linear) system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=-4x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}=x(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few trajectories (particular solutions) of the system are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe17a02809800422e4f5cfa0d6a906a57ce1b9de06344092a28decea37e81e23.png)\n\nNotice that the derivative $\\mathbf{x}'(t)$ can be interpreted as the *tangent vector* to the trajectory at the point $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\nIn the diagram above, the (normalized) tangents to the trajectories are shown as small gray vectors. \n\nAt each point in the phase plane, the system assigns a unique tangent vector. Taken together, these tangent vectors form the system’s **vector field**.\n\nIn our example above, suppose a trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2).$ Then, substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'} & =-4x+2y \\\\  & =-4(1)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =x-5y \\\\  & =(1)-5(-2) \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory at $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'} \\\\ {y}^{'}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168000 1064 "Consider a system of two differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\boldsymbol{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}f_{1}(x(t), y(t)) \\\\ f_{2}(x(t), y(t))\\end{bmatrix}$ is a vector-valued function of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$.\n\nIn this context, the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the **phase plane** (or **phase space**) of the system.\nAt each time $t$, the solution has a position\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich we view as a point $(x(t),y(t))$ in the phase plane. Now, as $t$ changes, the point $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ moves and traces out a curve in the phase plane.\nThis curve is called the **trajectory** (or **orbit**) of the solution.\n\nFor instance, consider the following (linear) system:\n\n$$\n{x^{′} =-4x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′} = x(t) - 5y(t)\n$$\n\nA few trajectories (particular solutions) of the system are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe17a02809800422e4f5cfa0d6a906a57ce1b9de06344092a28decea37e81e23.png)\n\nNotice that the derivative $\\mathbf{x}'(t)$ can be interpreted as the *tangent vector* to the trajectory at the point $\\mathbf{x}(t)$.\nIn the diagram above, the (normalized) tangents to the trajectories are shown as small gray vectors.\n\nAt each point in the phase plane, the system assigns a unique tangent vector. Taken together, these tangent vectors form the system’s **vector field**.\n\nIn our example above, suppose a trajectory (solution) passes through the point\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(1,-2)\n$$\n\nThen, substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′} &= -4x + 2y \\\\\n&=-4(1) + 2(-2) \\\\\n&=-8 \\\\\ny^{′} &= x - 5y \\\\\n&= (1) - 5(-2) \\\\\n&= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory at\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(1,-2)\n$$\n\nis\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′} \\\\ y^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168001 1064 "Again, consider an autonomous system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=g(x(t),y(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor which at a point $(x,y)$ in the phase plane, the tangent vector is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'} \\\\ {y}^{'}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can now define two special sets of points in the phase space:\n\n- The **$x$-nullcline** is the set of points where $x'=0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the $x$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {y}^{'}\\end{aligned}]$\nso the tangents to trajectories are *vertical*.\n\n- The **$y$-nullcline** is the set of points where $y'=0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nso the tangents to trajectories are *horizontal*.\n\nFor example, in the linear case, if the system is given as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}=ax(t)+by(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}=cx(t)+dy(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the nullclines are the straight lines with equations\n\n$$\nax+by=0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ncx+dy=0.\n$$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168001 1064 "Again, consider an autonomous system\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = f(x(t), y(t)); y^{′}(t) = g(x(t), y(t))\n$$\n\nfor which at a point $(x,y)$ in the phase plane, the tangent vector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′} \\\\ y^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can now define two special sets of points in the phase space:\n\n- The **$x$-nullcline** is the set of points where $x'=0$, i.e.\n$f(x,y)=0$.\nOn the $x$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ y^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\nso the tangents to trajectories are *vertical*.\n- The **$y$-nullcline** is the set of points where $y'=0$, i.e.\n$g(x,y)=0$.\nOn the $y$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nso the tangents to trajectories are *horizontal*.\n\nFor example, in the linear case, if the system is given as\n\n$$\n{x^{′} = ax(t) + by(t); y^{′} = cx(t) + dy(t)\n$$\n\nthen the nullclines are the straight lines with equations\n\n$$\nax+by=0 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad cx+dy=0\n$$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168002 1064 "A **phase portrait** is a diagram that shows the trajectories of a system along with their tangent directions.\n\nPhase portraits are usually generated with software. However, for simple linear systems, we can often sketch them by hand using the procedure below.\n\n- Start at a point $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- Draw a short line segment in the direction indicated by the direction field at that point. To determine the direction, we can use the vector field (if given) or compute the tangent vector at $(x_0,y_0),$ as we did earlier.\n\n- Move a small step in the direction we just drew.\n\nRepeat Steps 2 and 3, taking small steps and adjusting the direction each time to match the direction field at the new point.\n\nAs we sketch, we can use *nullclines* to help determine where the trajectories must turn:\n\n- On the $x$-nullcline (where $x'=0$), the trajectory is vertical.\n\n- On the $y$-nullcline (where $y'=0$), the trajectory is horizontal.\n\nFinally, remember that trajectories do not form sharp corners: they bend smoothly as the tangent direction changes.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168002 1064 "A **phase portrait** is a diagram that shows the trajectories of a system along with their tangent directions.\n\nPhase portraits are usually generated with software. However, for simple linear systems, we can often sketch them by hand using the procedure below.\n\n1. Start at a point $(x_0,y_0)$.\n2. Draw a short line segment in the direction indicated by the direction field at that point. To determine the direction, we can use the vector field (if given) or compute the tangent vector at $(x_0,y_0)$, as we did earlier.\n3. Move a small step in the direction we just drew.\n\nRepeat Steps 2 and 3, taking small steps and adjusting the direction each time to match the direction field at the new point.\n\nAs we sketch, we can use *nullclines* to help determine where the trajectories must turn:\n\n- On the $x$-nullcline (where $x'=0$), the trajectory is vertical.\n- On the $y$-nullcline (where $y'=0$), the trajectory is horizontal.\n\nFinally, remember that trajectories do not form sharp corners: they bend smoothly as the tangent direction changes.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168003 1064 "Consider a *decoupled* linear system of ODEs\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = λ_{1}x(t); y^{′}(t) = λ_{2}y(t)\n$$\n\nRecall that the eigenvalues for a diagonal matrix are its diagonal entries, $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$.\nThe corresponding eigenvectors are parallel to the coordinate axes:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, the general solution of the system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that if a solution starts on one of the coordinate axes, it remains on that axis. These are called **straight-line solutions**:\n\n- On the $x$-axis ($c_2=0$):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- On the $y$-axis ($c_1=0$):\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe stability of these solutions as $t\\to\\infty$ depends on the *signs* of the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$. We will explore the three main stability cases in the upcoming examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168003 1064 "Consider a *decoupled* linear system of ODEs\n![](images/3f/3f7a114fa75100f8cd3ccf72822758e668f3a6a0824ad5f5437ebef138928fb5.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we consider only decoupled systems with nonzero eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/b8/b8ed089264b88bdcf4018f44db6dade448063b56a6ded558fb74d7c2ca61c0de.svg) so the origin is the equilibrium point we will classify.\n\nRecall that the eigenvalues for a diagonal matrix are its diagonal entries, ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) \nThe corresponding eigenvectors are parallel to the coordinate axes:\n![](images/83/8337ebfc4aae4b8e97438cdba58872082bdc8965932659fdf2f8ff9cf061b375.svg)\n\nSo, the general solution of the system is given by\n![](images/fc/fcb5c1cea49dfe7aa1058f64d859a8b47c363f7f6195bf68056b18f1fa6d37f2.svg)\n\nNotice that if a solution starts on one of the coordinate axes, it remains on that axis. These are called **straight-line solutions**:\n\n- On the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis (![](images/8d/8da7ef7a61d4c5af4d28575fcd263714cf3bda8e7d94985bd4bea5c03eb2d53f.svg)): \n![](images/aa/aa9cd3c49609ee6d09a61c9c31438e4297620feedfda1073fbc55c08e7c53d01.svg)\n\n- On the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis (![](images/f2/f271ee76900762067d886b30871a9ea7538ae69318ad4514e5b39cfdce12644b.svg)):\n![](images/50/509154a662b215d445b282396a8f43828e582d43282467a0da73843ade7ec5d8.svg)\n\nThe stability of these solutions as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the *signs* of the eigenvalues ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/af/afb536c2c395100d85543d9c25f58d867e0bcfbd5f826d794284b648e7cc0550.svg) We will explore the three main stability cases in the upcoming examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168006 1064 "Sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-4$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny =-\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/14/1459d79913dec89577405a506b8d4adbbf4cb05abb51948eedae76d8f71544b8.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd53399b3d0c5ec40adcf91aaae4aca5ce55a9a7b574e9223fde615afc3b769f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bf/bf0c26af74326dd108d42eec1493b0ddf64cfdf0744c91b55e732b80b7d0835c.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168006 1064 "Sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 4y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t)\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=2\n$$\n\nfor which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny =-\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/14/1459d79913dec89577405a506b8d4adbbf4cb05abb51948eedae76d8f71544b8.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd53399b3d0c5ec40adcf91aaae4aca5ce55a9a7b574e9223fde615afc3b769f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bf/bf0c26af74326dd108d42eec1493b0ddf64cfdf0744c91b55e732b80b7d0835c.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168007 1064 "For a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = λ_{1}x(t); y^{′}(t) = λ_{2}y(t)\n$$\n\nwe know the *signs* of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ tell us the type of the equilibrium (sink/source/saddle). But how can we determine the shape of trajectories in cases when the origin is a source ($\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2 > 0$) or a sink ($\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2 < 0$)?\n\nTo do this, we can compare the rates of change of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ by looking at the slope of the tangent line to the trajectory,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nAssuming\n\n$$\nc_1,c_2\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\frac{\\lambda_2 y(t)}{\\lambda_1 x(t)} = \\frac{\\lambda_2 c_2}{\\lambda_1 c_1}\\,e^{(\\lambda_2-\\lambda_1)t}\n$$\n\nAs we can see, the long-term behavior of the slope as $t \\to \\infty$ depends on the sign of the exponent, $\\lambda_2-\\lambda_1$.\n\nThis comparison is governed by which eigenvalue has the larger magnitude. We call the eigenvalue with the largest absolute value the **dominant eigenvalue**.\n\nIn the next slides, we will analyze the source and sink cases separately." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168007 1064 "For a decoupled system\n![](images/df/df904ec5214399987c94f99323d3c3c4c06d9e19242c0d580574488011475acc.svg)\nwe know the *signs* of ![](images/77/77bce8fda831b5be2b2f52e043375447e794f8dad01a666d45e08716e912d707.svg) and ![](images/c0/c0413ea28006ddd6542ba7f9ebf2714b6091aca8fa79396d545b48db347c3a80.svg) tell us the type of the equilibrium (sink/source/saddle). But how can we determine the shape of trajectories in cases when the origin is a source (![](images/bb/bb6302eab08feb0614c62d4ea8012676675715a469dd30a7321ec228ff7ef4e9.svg)) or a sink (![](images/be/be8860e40a1c229262d3bf3375732f4b4583cad7aaf63f3a17e871bbd5a51abb.svg))?\n\nTo do this, we can compare the rates of change of ![](images/f7/f7dbfa03976240534a1b57f2cc59c474401a0d9524bc5f627736da01a0d775c1.svg) and ![](images/cc/cc436bde605f3822acb66d36528ba42a812a50fdbea361b31f0dbd0cba61d810.svg) by looking at the slope of the tangent line to the trajectory, ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg) Assuming ![](images/37/371aec72d3e326e3e52fbd1a1cfc06be06b33e3054e60b8c286a9fcbac39141e.svg) we have:\n![](images/be/be21baf0c518d85b105b0f569382fbc47075c268847437046b042db36a9b4b8a.svg)\n\nAs we can see, the long-term behavior of the slope as ![](images/91/919e9a7f2e074404c564846d6d8e1576e63b42ecbcc768b97d0632c645e32f22.svg) depends on the sign of the exponent, ![](images/7c/7cde6653feb562f0d136150bffa6f2925af0c7dbcb9ede5c7f1b3e0f10f3796d.svg)\n\nThis comparison is governed by which eigenvalue is larger. We call the larger eigenvalue (the one that is more positive, or less negative) the **dominant eigenvalue**.\n\nIn the next slides, we will analyze the source and sink cases separately." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168008 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2>0.$ \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ Since $\\lambda_2>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *unstable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called an **unstable node** or **source**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw:\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory for an *unstable node* emanates from the origin toward infinity. \n\n![](images/c9/c9f315c0b8a60a2c00e2f8e8e8b954214ce4d82cfd5832e15c26b429e29e535e.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168008 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = λ_{1}x(t); y^{′}(t) = λ_{2}y(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2>0$.\n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ Since $\\lambda_1>0$, we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$. Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Since $\\lambda_2>0$, we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$. Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *unstable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called an **unstable node** or **source**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw:\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), and\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory for an *unstable node* emanates from the origin toward infinity.\n\n![](images/c9/c9f315c0b8a60a2c00e2f8e8e8b954214ce4d82cfd5832e15c26b429e29e535e.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168009 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = λ_{1}x(t); y^{′}(t) = λ_{2}y(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2<0$.\n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ Since $\\lambda_1<0$, we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$. Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Since $\\lambda_2<0$, we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$. Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *stable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **stable node** or **sink**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin), and\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory flows into the origin.\n\n![](images/48/48fe66b9906af9dd53d0f24cacd1b6ffc2ca284960fa5e5d986b28beea5c1cec.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168009 1064 "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n![](images/3e/3e1a6630e46ba96ca21946d9feedc452d8416fe5bac17d5e8275c41c489d19ea.svg)\nwhere ![](images/78/7853d8d4de1afb7f8899c60d7a5ea8e5ecd281594e48536c9f2d0bbef2c0301b.svg)\n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/a1/a12ab1d3c69a589aa8de66fe8d14ed19b2938a9ef5591d87860e101a95afdc55.svg) Since ![](images/3d/3db8d2103adafd2be29df482295b07ef63576be6f152793463e034e808725edf.svg) we have that ![](images/bb/bb6223675110a379d33203ff637afaa6aefd3edf507b5a93386c608aa7aafca5.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis have the form ![](images/aa/aae0f8015e0a575ca1e1fdbb37d011cbf78574649e3c9874163c3f14f1ca9929.svg) Since ![](images/23/23d0a8291d49cc1d2f0c6cff735a4d5214c2d9079dca67d803846af9b063358f.svg) we have that ![](images/69/69c464920e94e968ac5b2fe07f52d5e8a5e694f0fab6c9bfdcfd1b359a82e89e.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) Thus, the trajectories on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis move *toward* the origin (*stable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *stable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **stable node** or **sink**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory flows into the origin.\n\n![](images/11/119ac25f56cfb326a9eb39eaf63c41e8497147c80b14da3a38e557e8aeb4f905.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168010 1064 "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a decoupled linear system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous linear system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$ \nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst of all, the coordinate axes are straight-line solutions:\n\n- On the $x$-axis, we have $y(t)= 0$ and $x(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}$.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ the $x$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ the $x$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\n- On the $y$-axis, we have $x(t)=0$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ the $y$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ the $y$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168010 1064 "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a decoupled linear system\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = λ_{1}x(t); y^{′}(t) = λ_{2}y(t)\n$$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous linear system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0)$.\nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$.\n\nFirst of all, the coordinate axes are straight-line solutions:\n\n- On the $x$-axis, we have $y(t)= 0$ and $x(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}$.If $\\lambda_1<0$, the $x$-axis is a *stable* line.If $\\lambda_1>0$, the $x$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n- On the $y$-axis, we have $x(t)=0$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}$.If $\\lambda_2<0$, the $y$-axis is a *stable* line.If $\\lambda_2>0$, the $y$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0$.\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0$. In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168011 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the following linear system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=2$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is an unstable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/b3/b32b5666c0138f5c17ff1622cef9bca6fb019375f6748b6422c11a547cfad282.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168011 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the following linear system:\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 2y(t)\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=2\n$$\n\nwith the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty$, so the origin is an unstable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/b3/b32b5666c0138f5c17ff1622cef9bca6fb019375f6748b6422c11a547cfad282.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168012 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) = y(t)\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ The vector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is such that\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$, the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}te^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= c_{1}e^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}e^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhen $c_2=1$, we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{x = te^{t}; y = e^{t}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-0.30$ | $0$ | $3.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.61$ | $1$ | $3.32$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168012 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ The vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.30$ | $0$ | $3.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.61$ | $1$ | $3.32$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168013 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{5t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c7/c7c824e97d40d2f982525040d5f0ba697c34c40ff51700ec3952d2909d566e21.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c3/c3030613b03a1afabd964d9494bf2359bd6ce5f021c731feda9607a81aaf5df6.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1\n$$\n\nthus, $\\lambda_1 = 5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43c6294fa7f68b21422eee71d35edd5f7880b3b3c9e23a2a8078090416cded05.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168013 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 4y(t)\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=5$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=2\n$$\n\nfor which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= 2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{5t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c7/c7c824e97d40d2f982525040d5f0ba697c34c40ff51700ec3952d2909d566e21.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ h