ven probability is to simply look it up in a **percentage points table**, such as the one below.\n\n| $\\quad p \\quad$ | $0.55$ | $0.60$ | $0.65$ | $0.70$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.1257\\,$ | $\\,0.2533\\,$ | $\\,0.3853\\,$ | $\\,0.5244\\,$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\nThis table gives the values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.\n\nLet's use the table above to find $z$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = 0.75\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given information in terms of $\\Phi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z \\le z) &= 0.75 \\\\\nΦ(z) &= 0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75)\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.75$, and we find that\n\n$$\np = 0.75 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(p) = 0.6745\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75) = 0.6745\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168110 1064 "To solve a second or third order homogeneous ODE, we can re-write it as an equivalent first order linear system of differential equations.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny''  -4 y' + e^t y= 0 , \\qquad y(1)= 1, \\quad y'(1)= 0\n$$\n\nwhere $y=y(t)$ is a function of $t.$\n\nFirst, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{'}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {y}^{'}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t) & ={y}^{'} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t) & ={y}^{''}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}={y}^{''}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 4y' - e^t y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 4x_2 - e^t x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}=-{e}^{t}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also write it in matrix form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:**\nIn the case of a higher order differential equation, the procedure is quite similar. For example, to convert a third-order linear differential equation into a system, we start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{'} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{''}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168110 1064 "To solve a second or third order homogeneous ODE, we can re-write it as an equivalent first order linear system of differential equations.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' -4 y' + e^t y= 0, \\qquad y(1)= 1, \\quad y'(1)= 0\n$$\n\nwhere $y=y(t)$ is a function of $t$.\n\nFirst, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(1) &= x_{1}(1) = 1 \\\\\ny^{′}(1) &= x_{2}(1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 4y' - e^t y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 4x_2 - e^t x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(1) = 1 \\\\ x_{2}^{′} =-e^{t}x_{1} + 4x_{2},\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(1) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can also write it in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′} \\\\ x_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note:**\nIn the case of a higher order differential equation, the procedure is quite similar. For example, to convert a third-order linear differential equation into a system, we start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{3} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168114 1064 "You might be wondering where the geometric distribution gets its name. The answer is that the probability mass function forms a geometric sequence!\n\nSuppose that $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$. Then, the probability mass function for this geometric distribution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= (1 - p)^{x - 1}p \\\\\nf(x) &= p(1 - p)^{x - 1} \\\\\nf(x)_(⏟)_(a_{x}) &= p_(⏟)_(a_{1}) \\cdot (1 - p_(⏟)_(r))^{x - 1} \\\\\na_{x} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{x - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we see that the probability mass function can be interpreted as a geometric sequence where the first term is $a_1=p$, and the common ratio is $r=1-p$.\n\nMoreover, notice that $f(x)$ satisfies all the criteria to be a probability mass function, namely\n\n1. $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nCriterion 1 is clearly true because\n\n$$\n0 \\leq p \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTo show that criterion 2 is true, we simply need to calculate the sum of this infinite geometric series, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(x ∈ S)f(x) &= ∑_(x = 1)^(∞)p(1 - p)^{x - 1} \\\\\n&= (p)/(1 - (1 - p)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{p}{p} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is indeed a probability distribution." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168114 1064 "You might be wondering where the geometric distribution gets its name. The answer is that the probability mass function forms a geometric sequence! \n\nSuppose that $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).$ Then, the probability mass function for this geometric distribution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-p)^{x-1}p \\\\ f(x) & =p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\ {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{x}} & ={{p}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}\\cdot ({{1-p}_{⏟}}_{r})^{x-1} \\\\ {a}_{x} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we see that the probability mass function can be interpreted as a geometric sequence where the first term is $a_1=p,$ and the common ratio is $r=1-p.$\n\nMoreover, notice that $f(x)$ satisfies all the criteria to be a probability mass function, namely\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nCriterion 1 is clearly true because $0 \\leq p \\leq 1.$ \n\nTo show that criterion 2 is true, we simply need to calculate the sum of this infinite geometric series, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x) & ={\\sum }_{x=1}^{\\infty }p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{1-(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{p} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is indeed a probability distribution." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168135 1064 "Suppose that we have a sequence of $n$ Bernoulli trials, each resulting in success or failure, and with a probability of success $p$. Let the random variable $X$ represent the number of successes.\n\nIt can be shown that $X$ follows a so-called **binomial distribution** and has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots, n\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- The factor $p^x$ represents the probability that exactly $x$ trials result in \"success.\"\n- The factor $(1-p)^{n-x}$ represents the probability that $n-x$ trials result in \"failure\".\n- The binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle\\binom{n}{x}$ counts the number of ways in which we can get exactly $x$ successes in $n$ trials. Remember that binomial coefficients are the same as combinations and can be computed as\n$\\displaystyle\\binom{n}{x} = {}_nC_x = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-x)!\\,x!}$.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution, we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim B(n,p)\n$$\n\nLet's go back to our first example and show that we get the same result." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168135 1064 "Suppose that we have a sequence of $n$ Bernoulli trials, each resulting in success or failure, and with a probability of success $p.$ Let the random variable $X$ represent the number of successes. \n\nIt can be shown that $X$ follows a so-called **binomial distribution** and has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots, n\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- The factor $p^x$ represents the probability that exactly $x$ trials result in \"success.\"\n\n- The factor $(1-p)^{n-x}$ represents the probability that $n-x$ trials result in \"failure\".\n\n- The binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle\\binom{n}{x}$ counts the number of ways in which we can get exactly $x$ successes in $n$ trials. Remember that binomial coefficients are the same as combinations and can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\binom{n}{x} = {}_nC_x = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-x)!\\,x!}.\n$$\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution, we write \n\n$$\nX\\sim B(n,p).\n$$\n\nLet's go back to our first example and show that we get the same result." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168136 1064 "Let $X$ be the number of heads obtained when a coin is tossed $\\color{blue}3$ times. Since we have a sequence of\n\n$$\nn=\\color{blue}3\n$$\n\nBernoulli trials, each with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nthe number of heads $X$ can be modeled as a binomial random variable, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left({\\color{blue}3}, \\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting $X=2$ heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 2) &= f(2) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{2})(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}(1 - \\frac{1}{2})^{3 - 2} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168136 1064 "Let $X$ be the number of heads obtained when a coin is tossed $\\color{blue}3$ times. Since we have a sequence of $n=\\color{blue}3$ Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success $p=\\dfrac12,$ the number of heads $X$ can be modeled as a binomial random variable, and we write \n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left({\\color{blue}3}, \\dfrac12\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting $X=2$ heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3\\,-\\,2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168137 1064 "A **Bernoulli trial** is a random experiment whose outcome can be interpreted as either \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nFor example, suppose we throw a die and define a \"success\" as the event of getting a six and a \"failure\" as getting any other number. Then, the throw of a die can be thought of as a Bernoulli trial with the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{six}) = \\dfrac16, \\qquad P(\\textrm{not six}) = \\dfrac56\n$$\n\nNow suppose that a fair die is repeatedly thrown *until* we get a six. What is the probability that we need to throw the die precisely ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ times to get our first six?\n\nFirst, notice that each throw of the die is a Bernoulli trial. And since we throw the die repeatedly until we get a six, we have a *sequence* of Bernoulli trials.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the die is thrown until we get a six. We wish to compute\n\n$$\nP(X = {\\color{blue}{3}})\n$$\n\nTo get our first six on the *third* throw, we need to throw \"not a six\" on the *first two* throws, followed by a \"six\" on the *final* throw. Assuming that each throw of the die is independent, by the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= P(not six) \\cdot P(not six) \\cdot P(six) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{216}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe random variable $X$ in this example follows a so-called **geometric distribution**. Let's now discuss geometric distributions a little more generally." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168137 1064 "A **Bernoulli trial** is a random experiment whose outcome can be interpreted as either \"success\" or \"failure.\" \n\nFor example, suppose we throw a die and define a \"success\" as the event of getting a six and a \"failure\" as getting any other number. Then, the throw of a die can be thought of as a Bernoulli trial with the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{six}) = \\dfrac16, \\qquad P(\\textrm{not six}) = \\dfrac56\n$$\n\nNow suppose that a fair die is repeatedly thrown *until* we get a six. What is the probability that we need to throw the die precisely ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ times to get our first six?\n\nFirst, notice that each throw of the die is a Bernoulli trial. And since we throw the die repeatedly until we get a six, we have a *sequence* of Bernoulli trials. \n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the die is thrown until we get a six. We wish to compute $P(X = {\\color{blue}{3}}).$ \n\nTo get our first six on the *third* throw, we need to throw \"not a six\" on the *first two* throws, followed by a \"six\" on the *final* throw. Assuming that each throw of the die is independent, by the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{six}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe random variable $X$ in this example follows a so-called **geometric distribution**. Let's now discuss geometric distributions a little more generally." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168138 1064 "Let $X$ be the number of times a fair die is thrown until we get a six. Since we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac16\n$$\n\nthe number of throws until the first six can be modeled as a geometric random variable, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac16\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting our first six on the $\\color{blue}3$rd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= f(3) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{1}{6})^{3 - 1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{5}{6})^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{36} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{216}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168138 1064 "Let $X$ be the number of times a fair die is thrown until we get a six. Since we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success $p=\\dfrac16,$ the number of throws until the first six can be modeled as a geometric random variable, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac16\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting our first six on the $\\color{blue}3$rd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168150 1064 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle xz + y,\\: x^2,\\: 2y-z^2 \\right\\rangle\\!, \\qquad \\mathbf{n}(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle 2,\\: 0,\\: -1 \\right\\rangle\\!\n$$\n\nLet's construct a surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nFirst, we recall that if $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨xz + y, x^{2}, 2y - z^{2}⟩ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨xz + y, x^{2}, 2y - z^{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}((xz + y) \\cdot 2 + x^{2} \\cdot 0 + (2y - z^{2}) \\cdot (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2xz + 2y - 2y + z^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2xz + z^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf n$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{2xz + z^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}dS \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}∬_(S)2xz + z^{2}dS\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168150 1064 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle xz + y,\\: x^2,\\: 2y-z^2 \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{n}(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle 2,\\: 0,\\: -1 \\right\\rangle\\!.\n$$\n\nLet's construct a surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}.$\n\nFirst, we recall that if $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xz+y,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨xz+y,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}((xz+y)\\cdot 2+{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+(2y-{z}^{2})\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2xz+2y-2y+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2xz+{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf n$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2xz+{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{∬}_{S}2xz+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168151 1064 "Recall that the flux of a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ across a curve $C$ with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y)$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nThe flux measures the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/23/2392d6c27fc6767c78172936d6df37b3dfec8b2198abbdf9e22b87d4337ed9da.png)\n\nFlux is always measured with respect to a preselected unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and we have two choices of orientation for this vector. If we measure the flux relative to the unit normal pointing in the *opposite* direction, our flux will have the *same* magnitude but *opposite* sign.\n\n![](images/e4/e451548a5d9874b9eff9d61c2d423696dc76a3f560e2936eb7bdb3a9cbfbed49.png)\n\nThis lesson aims to extend the concept of flux to three-dimensional vector fields. But first, we need a brief discussion on surface orientations." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168151 1064 "Recall that the flux of a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ across a curve $C$ with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y)$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux measures the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/23/2392d6c27fc6767c78172936d6df37b3dfec8b2198abbdf9e22b87d4337ed9da.png)\n\nFlux is always measured with respect to a preselected unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and we have two choices of orientation for this vector. If we measure the flux relative to the unit normal pointing in the *opposite* direction, our flux will have the *same* magnitude but *opposite* sign.\n\n![](images/e4/e451548a5d9874b9eff9d61c2d423696dc76a3f560e2936eb7bdb3a9cbfbed49.png)\n\nThis lesson aims to extend the concept of flux to three-dimensional vector fields. But first, we need a brief discussion on surface orientations." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168152 1064 "In three dimensions, flux measures the total flow of a vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ *through an orientable surface.*\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is a three-dimensional vector field, and $S$ is an orientable surface with the choice of orientation given by the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9976f35dcf348da89c15a40aad43ab5a1d3edea75558ef5539f9421ed9b75f4e.png)\n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol{S}$** with respect to $\\mathbf n$, which we write as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf S\n$$\n\nis given by the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n \\,\\textrm d S\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the flux formula for two-dimensional vector fields:\n\n- The quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ evaluated at a point $P$ on $S$ measures the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n$ at $P$.\n- By summing (integrating) the quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ over the entire surface, we get the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$.\n\nOur flux depends on our choice of orientation. If we were to select the *opposite* orientation, we'd get a result with the same magnitude but opposite sign." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168152 1064 "In three dimensions, flux measures the total flow of a vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ *through an orientable surface.*\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is a three-dimensional vector field, and $S$ is an orientable surface with the choice of orientation given by the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9976f35dcf348da89c15a40aad43ab5a1d3edea75558ef5539f9421ed9b75f4e.png)\n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol{S}$** with respect to $\\mathbf n,$ which we write as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf S\n$$\n\n is given by the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n \\,\\textrm d S.\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the flux formula for two-dimensional vector fields: \n\n- The quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ evaluated at a point $P$ on $S$ measures the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n$ at $P.$\n\n- By summing (integrating) the quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ over the entire surface, we get the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nOur flux depends on our choice of orientation. If we were to select the *opposite* orientation, we'd get a result with the same magnitude but opposite sign.\n\nAs in the case of two-dimensional vector fields, we have a choice between two possible normal vectors at each point. For instance, we could have picked the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction, as shown below.\n\n<div id=\"graphic-b46ee2d2f4637027c6704e244e0b2f96\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/b46ee2d2f4637027c6704e244e0b2f96\" /></div> \n\nThe key takeaway is that when calculating the flux of a vector field across a surface, we must be clear about which orientation we're measuring our flux relative to." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168153 1064 "A surface is **orientable** if it has two distinct sides. So, for example, the surface $S$ shown below is orientable.\n\n![](images/29/29be1e415eaf0072aea7f4093e71665f5996b29804702a37a4d4493e0b0002d4.png)\n\nIf a unit vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$ to our orientable surface varies continuously over $S$, we say that $S$ is **oriented** and the choice of $\\mathbf n$ gives an **orientation** of $S$.\n\nWe can choose the opposite orientation for our surface by selecting a vector that points in the opposite direction, as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40268c448b7c4ac99acf356e9f1e2702112a0d46dc1b6e6afd98d5c95e5ea317.png)\n\nWhen dealing with two-sided (i.e., orientable) surfaces, we must always be clear about our chosen orientation.\n\nNote that some surfaces are *not* orientable. This means they do not have two distinct sides. An example of a non-orientable surface is a Möbius strip, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/277ffb9e1594e64f3411b0c5063841616aaa433ccc4915bc96e2c9ab865ece95.png)\n\nA Möbius strip can be made by taking a long rectangular piece of paper, twisting one of its ends by $180^\\circ$ and gluing the two ends together.\n\nA Möbius strip does not have two distinct sides because if you were to start at a point $P$ on the strip and move along it (following the dark blue curve shown), you'd eventually end up at the same point but on the opposite side! Thus, a Möbius strip only has one distinct side.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider orientable surfaces." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168153 1064 "A surface is **orientable** if it has two distinct sides. So, for example, the surface $S$ shown below is orientable.\n\n![](images/29/29be1e415eaf0072aea7f4093e71665f5996b29804702a37a4d4493e0b0002d4.png)\n\nIf a unit vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$ to our orientable surface varies continuously over $S,$ we say that $S$ is **oriented** and the choice of $\\mathbf n$ gives an **orientation** of $S.$\n\nWe can choose the opposite orientation for our surface by selecting a vector that points in the opposite direction, as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40268c448b7c4ac99acf356e9f1e2702112a0d46dc1b6e6afd98d5c95e5ea317.png)\n\nWhen dealing with two-sided (i.e., orientable) surfaces, we must always be clear about our chosen orientation. \n\nNote that some surfaces are *not* orientable. This means they do not have two distinct sides. An example of a non-orientable surface is a Möbius strip, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/277ffb9e1594e64f3411b0c5063841616aaa433ccc4915bc96e2c9ab865ece95.png)\n\nA Möbius strip can be made by taking a long rectangular piece of paper, twisting one of its ends by $180^\\circ$ and gluing the two ends together. \n\nA Möbius strip does not have two distinct sides because if you were to start at a point $P$ on the strip and move along it (following the dark blue curve shown), you'd eventually end up at the same point but on the opposite side! Thus, a Möbius strip only has one distinct side. \n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider orientable surfaces." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168154 1064 "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k$, where $k$ is a fixed parameter.\n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid r_{x} \\cdot r_{y} \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂k}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂k}{∂y})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 0^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS &= ∬_(D)f(x, y, z) \\cdot \\mid \\mid r_{x} \\cdot r_{y} \\mid \\mid dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f(x, y, z) \\cdot 1dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f(x, y, z)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z) \\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168154 1064 "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k,$ where $k$ is a fixed parameter. \n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}|| & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we have that\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r_x =  \\langle 1, 0, 0\\rangle = \\mathbf i, \\qquad \\mathbf r_y =  \\langle 0, 1, 0\\rangle = \\mathbf j.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf r_x \\times\\mathbf r_y = \\mathbf i\\times\\mathbf j = \\mathbf k\n\\]\n\nand therefore\n\n\\[\n|| \\mathbf r_x \\times\\mathbf r_y|| = ||\\mathbf k|| =1. \n\\]\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,f(x,y,z)\\text{d}S\\, & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168160 1064 "Suppose we want to find the *maximum* value of the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 + 4x_2^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1\n$$\n\nThis type of problem is known as a **constrained optimization problem**.\n\nSince our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ has no mixed term $x_1x_2$, we can find its maximum value for\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\mid \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nusing algebraic manipulation, as follows.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nx_1^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 4x_1^2\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 2x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&\\le 4x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 4(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\nx_1^2 + x_2^2 =1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n4(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}) = 4(1) = 4\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 4\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $4$. But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to make sure that $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can actually attain the value $4$. Can we find a vector $\\mathbf x$, with\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\mid \\mid = 1\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = 4\n$$\n\nIndeed, we can. Setting $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 2x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2(0)^{2} + 4(1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1\n$$\n\nthe maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ equals $4$.\n\n\nProceeding similarly, we can show that the minimum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $2$.\n\nGraphically, the situation can be visualized if we intersect the surface\n\n$$\nz= {\\color{black}2x_1^2+4x_2^2}\n$$\n\nand the cylinder\n\n$$\n\\color{black}x_1^2+x_2^2=1\n$$\n\nas shown below:\n\n![](images/39/39bfc05c9dd0733c1f4f867d21d1a5f018aacbf0ed91036a6f0981d701ba69d8.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168160 1064 "Suppose we want to find the *maximum* value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 + 4x_2^2,\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraint $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1.$ This type of problem is known as a **constrained optimization problem**.   \n\nOur constraint means we only consider vectors that lie on the unit sphere centered at the origin. \n\nSince our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ has no mixed term $x_1x_2$, we can find its maximum value for $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1$ using algebraic manipulation, as follows.\n\nFirst, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 4x_1^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=4(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to make sure that $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can actually attain the value $4.$ Can we find a vector $\\mathbf x,$ with  $\\| \\mathbf{x} || = 1,$ such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = 4?$\n\nIndeed, we can. Setting $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$  the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ equals $4.$\n\nProceeding similarly, we can show that the minimum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $2.$ \n\nGraphically, the situation can be visualized if we intersect the surface $z= {\\color{black}2x_1^2+4x_2^2}$ and the cylinder $\\color{black}x_1^2+x_2^2=1,$ as shown below:  \n\n![](images/39/39bfc05c9dd0733c1f4f867d21d1a5f018aacbf0ed91036a6f0981d701ba69d8.png)" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186168161 1064 "We can find a quadratic form's maximum and minimum values even when it contains cross-product terms. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$:*\n> - *The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* $\\max \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$*The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$.*\n> - *The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* $\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$*The minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}$.*\n\nSuppose we are given the quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=4x_1^2-2x_1x_2+4x_2^2\n$$\n\nHow can we find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ at which the maximum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is attained?\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)(4 - λ) - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 8λ + 15 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 5)(λ - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 5, 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\max \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\ &= 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$, we need a unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5\n$$\n\nWe start by computing $(A-5I)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 5I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of\n\n$$\n(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ngives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186168161 1064 "We can find a quadratic form's maximum and minimum values even when it contains cross-product terms. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin{:}$*\n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> - *The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n> \n> $$\n> \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> *The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$ *\n> \n> - *The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n> \n> $$\n> \\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> \n> *The minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$ *\n\nSuppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=4x_1^2-2x_1x_2+4x_2^2.\n$$\n\nHow can we find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ at which the maximum of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is attained?\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = 5.\n$$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}},$ we need a unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5.$ We start by computing $(A-5I)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186176995 1064 "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nNow, if our surface $S$ is defined as $z= f(x,y),$ then it can always be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x,y)= \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: f(x,y)\\right\\rangle  \\qquad (x,y)  \\in D.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variables $x$ and $y$ act as parameters. \n\nThus, the fundamental vector product of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{∂f}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂f}{∂y},\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand using the fact that $z = f(x,y),$ we can write this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'} & =⟨-\\frac{∂z}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂z}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| = \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that for a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to apply this formula." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186176995 1064 "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nNow, if our surface $S$ is defined as $z= f(x,y)$, then it can always be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x,y)= \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: f(x,y)\\right\\rangle \\qquad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variables $x$ and $y$ act as parameters.\n\nThus, the fundamental vector product of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\frac{∂f}{∂x},-\\frac{∂f}{∂y}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand using the fact that $z = f(x,y)$, we can write this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} &= ⟨ - \\frac{∂z}{∂x},-\\frac{∂z}{∂y}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that for a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to apply this formula." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186176996 1064 "![](images/86/867722106ffd8445b62a98ab43197ac2fba646faff95017ef7e92c254a97fb6e.png)\n\nThe surface $S$ shown above consists of the part of the paraboloid\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nthat lies under the plane $z=9$. Let's compute the surface area of $S$.\n\nThe plane intersects the paraboloid when $z=9$. Thus, the curve that describes the intersection points is\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTherefore, our surface can be described by the equations\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2, \\quad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): \\, x^2 + y^2 \\le 9\\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo compute the surface area of $S$, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2x, \\\\\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our surface area formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{∂z}{∂x})^{2} + (\\frac{∂z}{∂y})^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + (2x)^{2} + (2y)^{2}}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{2} + 4y^{2}}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by changing the variables to polar coordinates $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm dA = r\\,\\textrm d r\\, \\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThus, our integration region becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta): \\, 0\\leq r\\leq 3,\\:0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi\\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{2} + 4y^{2}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\sqrt{1 + 4(r\\cos θ)^{2} + 4(r\\sin θ)^{2}}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∬_(Δ)r\\sqrt{1 + 4r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ)}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∬_(Δ)r\\sqrt{1 + 4r^{2}}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1 + 4r^{2}}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}dθ\\int_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1 + 4r^{2}}dr \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1 + 4r^{2}}dr \\\\\n&= 2π[\\frac{1}{12}(1 + 4r^{2})^{3/2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}[(1 + 4r^{2})^{3/2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}(37\\sqrt{37} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the area of our surface is\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(37\\sqrt{37} -1)\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186176996 1064 "![](images/86/867722106ffd8445b62a98ab43197ac2fba646faff95017ef7e92c254a97fb6e.png)\n\nThe surface $S$ shown above consists of the part of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ that lies under the plane $z=9.$ Let's compute the surface area of $S.$\n\nThe plane intersects the paraboloid when $z=9.$ Thus, the curve that describes the intersection points is\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 9,\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nTherefore, our surface can be described by the equations\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2, \\quad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): \\, x^2 + y^2 \\le 9\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the surface area of $S,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our surface area formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by changing the variables to polar coordinates $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta),$ we have $\\textrm dA = r\\,\\textrm d r\\, \\textrm d \\theta.$ Thus, our integration region becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta): \\, 0\\leq r\\leq 3,\\:0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+4(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta )}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,d\\theta \\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{1}{12}{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(37\\sqrt{37}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the area of our surface is \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(37\\sqrt{37} -1).\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186177025 1064 "The fundamental theorem of algebra tells us that any quartic polynomial with real coefficients has precisely four roots.\n\nMoreover, since complex roots always come in complex conjugate pairs, if $p(x)$ is a quartic polynomial with real coefficients, we must have one of the following cases:\n\n- $p(x)$ has $4$ real roots (some of which might be repeated).\n- $p(x)$ has $2$ (possibly repeated) real roots and $2$ complex conjugate roots. For example,\n$p(x)|= x^{4} - 1; = (x^{2} - 1)(x^{2} + 1); = (x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\textrm{i})(x + \\textrm{i})$.\nIn this case, the real roots are $x=\\pm 1$, and the complex conjugate roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i$.\n\n- $p(x)$ has two pairs of complex conjugate roots. For example,\n$p(x)|= (x^{2} + 1)(x^{2} + 4); = (x - \\textrm{i})(x + \\textrm{i})(x - 2\\textrm{i})(x + 2\\textrm{i})$.\nIn this case, the complex conjugate pairs of roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i$ and $x=\\pm 2\\textrm i$. Also, notice that it's possible for the complex conjugate pairs of roots to be repeated. For example,\n$p(x)|= x^{4} + 2x^{2} + 1; = (x^{2} + 1)^{2}; = ((x - \\textrm{i})(x + \\textrm{i}))^{2}; = (x - \\textrm{i})(x - \\textrm{i})(x + \\textrm{i})(x + \\textrm{i})$.\nIn this case, the roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i$. Each of these roots is a double root." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186177025 1064 "The fundamental theorem of algebra tells us that any quartic polynomial with real coefficients has precisely four roots. \n\nMoreover, since complex roots always come in complex conjugate pairs, if $p(x)$ is a quartic polynomial with real coefficients, we must have one of the following cases:\n\n- $p(x)$ has $4$ real roots (some of which might be repeated).\n\n- $p(x)$ has $2$ (possibly repeated) real roots and $2$ complex conjugate roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1)(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the real roots are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the complex conjugate roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i.$\n\n- $p(x)$ has two pairs of complex conjugate roots. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i})(x-\\text{2}\\text{i})(x+\\text{2}\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the complex conjugate pairs of roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i$ and $x=\\pm 2\\textrm i.$ \n\n Also, notice that it's possible for the complex conjugate pairs of roots to be repeated. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={((x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i}))}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-\\text{i})(x-\\text{i})(x+\\text{i})(x+\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the roots are $x=\\pm\\textrm i.$ Each of these roots is a double root." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186177081 1064 "We have similar formulas for the areas of surfaces defined by $x = f(y,z)$ and\n\n$$\ny = f(x,z){:}\n$$\n\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A$,\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\, \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A$,\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186177081 1064 "We have similar formulas for the areas of surfaces defined by $x = f(y,z)$ and $y = f(x,z){:}$\n\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186189366 1064 "To find the first derivative of a parametric curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nA formula for the second derivative of a parametric curve can be found by applying the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\cdot \\frac{dθ}{dx} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{d}{dθ}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dθ}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186189366 1064 "To find the first derivative of a parametric curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nA formula for the second derivative of a parametric curve can be found by applying the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})\\cdot \\frac{d\\theta }{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186189374 1064 "It's often helpful to use the double-angle formula for sine when computing the slope of the tangent to a polar curve, as this often makes the differentiation easier.\n\nLet's consider the polar curve\n\n$$\nr = 1-2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below\n\n![](images/53/53c660d25a8bec2346cacd95d70fb3f4ee27afe039bd23cbe7affa3cf04eb09a.png)\n\nRecall that the slope of the tangent (in terms of $\\theta$) is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nr = 1-2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (1 - 2\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ - 2\\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= (1 - 2\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\sin θ - 2\\sin θ\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\sin θ - \\sin 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\color{black}\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nto simplify our expression for $y$.\n\nTo compute our derivative, we need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$.\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (\\cos θ - 2\\cos^{2} θ)^{′}; = (\\cos θ)^{′} - (2\\cos^{2} θ)^{′}; = (-\\sin θ) - (2 \\cdot 2\\cos θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ)); =-\\sin θ + 2 \\cdot 2\\sin θ\\cos θ; =-\\sin θ + 2 \\cdot \\sin 2θ; = 2\\sin 2θ - \\sin θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (\\sin θ - \\sin 2θ)^{′}; = (\\sin θ)^{′} - (\\sin 2θ)^{′}; = \\cos θ - 2\\cos 2θ$\n\nFinally, the slope of the tangent is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\cos θ - 2\\cos 2θ}{2\\sin 2θ - \\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186189374 1064 "It's often helpful to use the double-angle formula for sine when computing the slope of the tangent to a polar curve, as this often makes the differentiation easier.\n\nLet's consider the polar curve $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ whose graph is shown below \n\n![](images/53/53c660d25a8bec2346cacd95d70fb3f4ee27afe039bd23cbe7affa3cf04eb09a.png)\n\nRecall that the slope of the tangent (in terms of $\\theta$) is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2{cos}^{2}\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the double-angle formula $\\color{black}\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ to simplify our expression for $y.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2{cos}^{2}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{'}-(2{cos}^{2}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )-(2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta )}^{'} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{'}-{(\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta )}^{'} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the slope of the tangent is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(\\theta )}{{x}^{'}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }{2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199460 1064 "Recall that a lamina's *center of mass* is the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$ where its total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the coordinate axes as the original lamina.\n\nThe center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\\:$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nRemember that if we could place a fulcrum under our lamina at its center of mass, it would balance perfectly." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199460 1064 "Recall that a lamina's *center of mass* is the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$ where its total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the coordinate axes as the original lamina. \n\nThe center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\\:$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nRemember that if we could place a fulcrum under our lamina at its center of mass, it would balance perfectly." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199461 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin lamina whose mass is unevenly distributed.\n\nWe describe the mass distribution across a lamina using its mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$. This function tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the lamina.\n\nFor example, consider a thin plate with mass density function\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = 6xy\n$$\n\noccupying the finite rectangular region $D$ between the lines $x=1$, $x=2$, $y=0$, and $y=1$. What is the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $(x,y) \\in D$ on the plate and a small rectangular piece with its bottom-left corner at $(x,y)$ and area $\\Delta{A}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cd947e542aaf7eb787abc7a846e87a2d03ccafa0987f3422928fdcba115da9e.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{A}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the moment of this piece about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\nΔM_{y} \\mid \\approx x \\cdot Δm; \\approx x \\cdot 6xyΔA; = 6x^{2}yΔA\n$$\n\nTo find the total moment of the plate about the axis, we add together the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta{A} \\to 0$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nM_y = \\iint\\limits_D 6x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{y} &= ∬_(D)6x^{2}ydA \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}6x^{2}ydydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}3x^{2}dx \\cdot \\int_{0}^{1}2ydy \\\\\n&= [x^{3}]_{x = 1}^{x = 2} \\cdot [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 1} \\\\\n&= (8 - 1) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe moment about the $x$-axis can be found similarly.\n\nIn general, the moments $M_x$ and $M_y$ of a lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis and about the $y$-axis, respectively, are given by the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{x} &= ∬_(D)yλ(x, y)dA \\\\\nM_{y} &= ∬_(D)xλ(x, y)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199461 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin lamina whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution across a lamina using its mass density function $\\lambda (x,y).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the lamina.\n\nFor example, consider a thin plate with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 6xy$ occupying the finite rectangular region $D$ between the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ What is the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $(x,y) \\in D$ on the plate and a small rectangular piece with its bottom-left corner at $(x,y)$ and area $\\Delta{A},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cd947e542aaf7eb787abc7a846e87a2d03ccafa0987f3422928fdcba115da9e.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{A}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the moment of this piece about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{y} & \\approx x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & \\approx x\\cdot 6xy\\,ΔA \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y\\,ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total moment of the plate about the axis, we add together the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta{A} \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nM_y =  \\iint\\limits_D 6x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe moment about the $x$-axis can be found similarly.\n\nIn general, the moments $M_x$ and $M_y$ of a lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis and about the $y$-axis, respectively, are given by the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199462 1064 "When a lamina's mass density is constant, the center of mass depends only on the shape of the lamina. In cases like these, the center of mass is called the **centroid** of the lamina. The centroid of a shape corresponds to its geometrical center.\n\nSo, $\\lambda$ is constant, then the mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ∬_{D}λdA \\\\\n&= λ∬_{D}dA \\\\\n&= λ \\cdot A_{D}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A_D$ denotes the area of $D$. As a result,\n\n$$\nm = \\lambda \\cdot A_D \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{\\lambda}{m} = \\dfrac{1}{A_D}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the formula for the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{M_{y}}{m} &= \\frac{1}{m}∬_(D)xλdA \\\\\n&= \\frac{λ}{m}∬_(D)xdA \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{A_{D}}∬_(D)xdA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can similarly compute the $y$-coordinate.\n\nIn general, the centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c)$, where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199462 1064 "When a lamina's mass density is constant, the center of mass depends only on the shape of the lamina. In cases like these, the center of mass is called the **centroid** of the lamina. The centroid of a shape corresponds to its geometrical center.\n\nSo, $\\lambda$ is constant, then the mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda \\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda {∬}_{D}\\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda \\cdot {A}_{D},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A_D$ denotes the area of $D.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nm = \\lambda \\cdot A_D \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{\\lambda}{m} = \\dfrac{1}{A_D}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the formula for the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m} & =\\frac{1}{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda \\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\lambda }{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can similarly compute the $y$-coordinate.\n\nIn general, the centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c),$ where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \n\\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199471 1064 "Recall that the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a smooth, orientable surface $S$ with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}$ is a unit normal vector that defines the orientation of the surface.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa63a1e4f1d278cef6c53600e8fb9c60829de972ba7409afdc32837ecbc3e07.png)\n\nIf $S$ is given by a parametric equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v), \\qquad (u,v)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nwe can determine the unit normal vector using the fundamental product as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v}{\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\|}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the formula for the flux, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)F \\cdot \\frac{r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}}{‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖}dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)F(r(u, v)) \\cdot \\frac{r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}}{‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖}‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)F(r(u, v)) \\cdot (r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $S$ is a smooth, orientable surface given in parametric form by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v)\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, then the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf F(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199471 1064 "Recall that the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a smooth, orientable surface $S$ with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} \\,\\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}$ is a unit normal vector that defines the orientation of the surface.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa63a1e4f1d278cef6c53600e8fb9c60829de972ba7409afdc32837ecbc3e07.png)\n\nIf $S$ is given by a parametric equation of the form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v), \\qquad (u,v)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,\n$$\n\nwe can determine the unit normal vector using the fundamental product as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v}{\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\|}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into the formula for the flux, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}}{‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}}{‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖}\\,‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $S$ is a smooth, orientable surface given in parametric form by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D  \\mathbf F(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199475 1064 "![](images/d2/d2a5641e502b11cca4f469ec743adc62d614f669d61c1b2d0b0c7549fb60bf6b.png)\n\nGiven a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin, an outward-pointing unit normal vector to the sphere at the point $P(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= \\frac{⟨x, y, z⟩}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sometimes use this to calculate the flux of a vector field through a sphere. Let's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199475 1064 "![](images/d2/d2a5641e502b11cca4f469ec743adc62d614f669d61c1b2d0b0c7549fb60bf6b.png)\n\nGiven a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin, an outward-pointing unit normal vector to the sphere at the point $P(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sometimes use this to calculate the flux of a vector field through a sphere. Let's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199476 1064 "Let's now derive a general formula for the flux through an *upward-oriented* Cartesian surface.\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider a surface $S$ given by the equation $z=g(x,y)$. The canonical parameterization of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: g(x,y) \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nComputing the tangent vectors to the grid curves, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} &= ⟨1, 0, \\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\\nr_{y}^{′} &= ⟨0, 1, \\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= ⟨ - \\frac{∂g}{∂x},-\\frac{∂g}{∂y}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the $\\mathbf k$-component is positive, which indicates that our surface is upward-oriented.\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)F(r(x, y)) \\cdot (r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)⟨P, Q, R⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - \\frac{∂g}{∂x},-\\frac{∂g}{∂y}, 1⟩dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize as follows:\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(-P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A$.\n\nWe have analogous results for surfaces of the form $y = h(x,z)$ and $x=k(y,z){:}$\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(-P\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - R\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A$,\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf j$-component is positive.*\n- *For a surface $S$ given $x = k(y,z)$ for $(y,z) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(P - Q\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial y} - R\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A$,\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf i$-component is positive.*" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199476 1064 "Let's now derive a general formula for the flux through an *upward-oriented* Cartesian surface.\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nConsider a surface $S$ given by the equation $z=g(x,y).$ The canonical parameterization of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: g(x,y) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the tangent vectors to the grid curves, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the $\\mathbf k$-component is positive, which indicates that our surface is upward-oriented.\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize as follows:\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe have analogous results for surfaces of the form $y = h(x,z)$ and $x=k(y,z){:}$\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - R\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf j$-component is positive.*\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given $x = k(y,z)$ for $(y,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( P - Q\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial y} - R\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf i$-component is positive.*" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199479 1064 "Let's calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle (x-y)^2, \\:(x+y)^2, \\: 12xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nthrough the surface $S$ defined by the Cartesian equation \n\n$$\nz=2y-2x\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ \n\nFor surfaces like this, substituting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a parametrization \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: 2v-2u \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨(x-y)^{2},\\,(x+y)^{2},\\,12xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2v-2u} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2},\\,12uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2},\\,12uv⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)^{2}\\cdot 2+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2)+12uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x-y)^{2},\\,(x+y)^{2},\\,12xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[{u}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199479 1064 "Let's calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle (x-y)^2, \\:(x+y)^2, \\: 12xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the surface $S$ defined by the Cartesian equation\n\n$$\nz=2y-2x\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nFor surfaces like this, substituting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain a parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: 2v-2u \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nRecall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v - 2u)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0,-2⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v - 2u)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2,-2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(u, v)) &= \\left.⟨(x - y)^{2}, (x + y)^{2}, 12xy⟩\\right|_{x = u, y = v, z = 2v - 2u} \\\\\n&= ⟨(u - v)^{2}, (u + v)^{2}, 12uv⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(u, v)) \\cdot (r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}) &= ⟨(u - v)^{2}, (u + v)^{2}, 12uv⟩ \\cdot ⟨2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= (u - v)^{2} \\cdot 2 + (u + v)^{2} \\cdot (-2) + 12uv \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 4uv\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)⟨(x - y)^{2}, (x + y)^{2}, 12xy⟩ \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)F(r(u, v)) \\cdot (r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4uvdA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}4uvdvdu \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}2udu \\cdot \\int_{0}^{1}2vdv \\\\\n&= [u^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [v^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (1^{2} - 0^{2}) \\cdot (1^{2} - 0^{2}) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199485 1064 "Similar to the case of *closed* curves, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" a closed *surface*.\n\nFor closed surfaces, the convention is the positive orientation is always given by the *outward-pointing* unit normal vector.\n\n![](images/d2/d2bb07e4684cfc3a8c5641ecc6930dbc1c76027a735c7efe0c1c60cd17a91060.png)\n\nWhen calculating the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a closed surface, we always measure the flux relative to the surface's positive orientation.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199485 1064 "Similar to the case of *closed* curves, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" a closed *surface*. \n\nFor closed surfaces, the convention is the positive orientation is always given by the *outward-pointing* unit normal vector.\n\n![](images/d2/d2bb07e4684cfc3a8c5641ecc6930dbc1c76027a735c7efe0c1c60cd17a91060.png)\n\nWhen calculating the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a closed surface, we always measure the flux relative to the surface's positive orientation.  \n\nLet's see an example." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199486 1064 "Let's derive the formula for a unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin.\n\nConsider the function\n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - r^2\\right)\n$$\n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $S$ with equation\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nConsider the point $P(x,y,z)$ that lies on $S$. Then, $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ is a normal vector to the level surface at the point $P$.\n\nComputing $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= ⟨f_{x}, f_{y}, f_{z}⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}⟨2x, 2y, 2z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨x, y, z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P(x,y,z)$ lies on $S$, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nNormalizing $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (∇f(x, y, z))/(‖∇f(x, y, z)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨x, y, z⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨x, y, z⟩}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= \\frac{⟨x, y, z⟩}{r}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199486 1064 "Let's derive the formula for a unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin. \n\nConsider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - r^2\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2.$ \n\nConsider the point $P(x,y,z)$ that lies on $S.$ Then, $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ is a normal vector to the level surface at the point $P$.\n\nComputing $\\nabla f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨{f}_{x},\\,{f}_{y},\\,{f}_{z}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P(x,y,z)$ lies on $S,$ we have $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2.$\nNormalizing $\\nabla f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{∇f(x,y,z)}{‖∇f(x,y,z)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199493 1064 "We've spent some time studying so-called **plane transformations** of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll generalize our understanding of derivatives to transformations (or functions) of the form\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m\n$$\n\nTo motivate the discussion, we start by considering functions of one variable.\n\nRecall that a function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is **differentiable** at a point $x = a$ if there exists a number $f'(a)$, called the **derivative** of $f$ at $a$, such that\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}\n$$\n\nWe can restate this definition by applying basic algebraic properties of limits.\n\nSince the left-hand side, $f'(a)$, is just a constant, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} f'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}\n$$\n\nusing the fact that the limit of a constant is the constant itself.\n\nBringing all terms to one side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left(\\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} - f'(a) \\right) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich can be rearranged as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left(\\frac{f(x) - f(a) - f'(a)(x - a)}{x - a} \\right) = 0\n$$\n\nThis final expression tells us that the difference between $f(x)$ and its *linear approximation* $f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ becomes negligible relative to $x - a$ as $x$ approaches $a$.\n\nIn other words, not only does $f(x)$ become close to $f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)$ near $a$, but the error between them shrinks *faster than* the distance $\\mid x - a \\mid$ itself.\n\nLet's see how we can generalize this definition to vector-valued functions." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199493 1064 "We've spent some time studying so-called **plane transformations** of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll generalize our understanding of derivatives to transformations (or functions) of the form \n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.\n$$\n\nTo motivate the discussion, we start by considering functions of one variable. \n\nRecall that a function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is **differentiable** at a point $x = a$ if there exists a number $f'(a)$, called the **derivative** of $f$ at $a$, such that\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nWe can restate this definition by applying basic algebraic properties of limits.\n\nSince the left-hand side, $f'(a)$, is just a constant, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} f'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a},\n$$\n\nusing the fact that the limit of a constant is the constant itself.\n\nBringing all terms to one side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} - f'(a) \\right) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rearranged as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a) - f'(a)(x - a)}{x - a} \\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis final expression tells us that the difference between $f(x)$ and its *linear approximation* $f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ becomes negligible relative to $x - a$ as $x$ approaches $a$.\n\nIn other words, not only does $f(x)$ become close to $f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)$ near $a$, but the error between them shrinks *faster than* the distance $|x - a|$ itself.\n\nSo, in a neighborhood of [math]x=a,[/math] the change in the value of the function [math]\\Delta{f}[/math] is approximately proportional to the change in the value of the argument [math]\\Delta{x}.[/math] The coefficient of the proportionality is the derivative [math]{\\color{blue}{f'(a)}}.[/math]\n\nLet's see how we can generalize this definition to vector-valued functions." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199494 1064 "The equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$, where\n\n$$\nz_0=f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nLet's express this equation in terms of the total derivative.\n\nWriting our multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R$ as\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\mathbf x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ the total derivative of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{0} \\\\ y_{0}\\end{bmatrix}$ Then, we can re-write the equation of the tangent plane as a matrix equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - z_{0} &= (x - x_{0}) \\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂x}(x_{0}, y_{0}) + (y - y_{0}) \\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(x_{0}, y_{0}) \\\\\nz - z_{0} &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(x_{0}, y_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(x_{0}, y_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x - x_{0} \\\\ y - y_{0}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nz - f(x_{0}, y_{0}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(x_{0}, y_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(x_{0}, y_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}x_{0} \\\\ y_{0}\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\nz - f(a) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(a) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(a)\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (x - a) \\\\\nz - f(a) &= f^{′}(a)(x - a)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane to $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nin matrix form, is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199494 1064 "The equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nLet's express this equation in terms of the total derivative.\n\nWriting our multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R$ as $f\\left(\\mathbf x \\right),$ where $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ the total derivative of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}]$ Then, we can re-write the equation of the tangent plane as a matrix equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-{z}_{0} & =(x-{x}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) \\\\ z-{z}_{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-{x}_{0} \\\\ y-{y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z-f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ z-f(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(a) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (x-a) \\\\ z-f(a) & ={f}^{'}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane to $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a},$ in matrix form, is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the last equation, [math]f(\\mathbf{a})[/math] is a scalar, [math]\\mathbf{x},[/math] [math]\\mathbf{a},[/math] and [math]\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a},[/math] are [math][2 \\times 1][/math] column vector, while [math]\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)[/math] is a [math][1 \\times 2][/math] row vector. So, the dimensions are consistent. We have a scalar on the left-hand side and [math][1 \\times 2] \\cdot [2 \\times 1] = [1 \\times 1][/math] scalar on the right-hand side." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199495 1064 "If $f:\\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R$ is differentiable at $x=a$, then for points close to $x=a$, we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for transformations of the form $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a\n$$\n\nthen for points close to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a\n$$\n\nwe have the linear approximation\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf x) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nNotice that this approximation for $\\boldsymbol f$ is affine. Thus, if a function is differentiable at a point\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nit is **locally affine** in a neighborhood of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199495 1064 "If $f:\\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R$ is differentiable at $x=a,$ then for points close to $x=a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\nthe tangent line to [math]y=f(x)[/math] at [math]x=a[/math] is given by\n\n\\[\ny-f(a) = f'(a)(x-a)\n\\]\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx f(a) + f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for transformations of the form $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.$\n\nThe **tangent hyperplane** to the hypersurface [math]\\mathbf{y} = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})[/math] at [math]\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a[/math] is given by\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf y - \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)\n\\] \n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ then for points close to $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf x) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this approximation for $\\boldsymbol f$ is affine. Thus, if a function is differentiable at a point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a,$ it is **locally affine** in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ \n\n**Note:** In the last two equations, the dimensions are consistent. We have an [math][m \\times 1][/math] column vector on the left-hand side and [math][m \\times n] \\cdot [n \\times 1] = [m \\times 1][/math] column vector on the right-hand side.\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199501 1064 "Suppose that a transformation (or function) $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions of $n$ variables.\nWe say that $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is **differentiable** at a point\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\nif there exists an $m \\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})$, called the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{a}$, such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{a}} \\frac{\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) - \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) - \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})}{\\mid \\mid \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a} \\mid \\mid} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn other words, near $\\mathbf{a}$, the function $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is well-approximated by the linear map\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nand the error between $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ and this linear approximation becomes negligible compared to the distance $\\|\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}\\|$ as $\\mathbf{x}$ approaches $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x}$, then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f_{m}}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes refer to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ simply as **the derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f(\\mathbf x)$.\n- For scalar-valued functions of the form $f: \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$, the total derivative is the *transpose* of the gradient vector:\n$\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a) = \\left(\\nabla f \\right)^T(\\mathbf a)$\n- For vector-valued functions $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of a single parameter $t$, the total derivative at $t=a$ is the usual column vector containing the partial derivatives of the components of $f{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂t} \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂t} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{n}}{∂t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- For functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2$, the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian:\n$\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right) = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2)}$\nSimilarly, for functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$, the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian:\n$\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right) = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2,f_3)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2,x_3)}$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199501 1064 "Suppose that a transformation (or function) $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions of $n$ variables. \nWe say that $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is **differentiable** at a point $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ if there exists an $m \\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})$, called the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{a}$, such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{a}}\n\\frac{\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) - \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) - \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n}{\n||\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}||\n}\n= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, near $\\mathbf{a}$, the function $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is well-approximated by the linear map\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}),\n$$\n\nand the error between $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ and this linear approximation becomes negligible compared to the distance $\\|\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}\\|$ as $\\mathbf{x}$ approaches $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes refer to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ simply as **the derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f(\\mathbf x).$\n\n- For scalar-valued functions of the form $f: \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R,$ the total derivative is the *transpose* of the gradient vector:\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a) = \\left(\\nabla f \\right)^T(\\mathbf a)\n$$\n\n- For vector-valued functions $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of a single parameter $t,$ the total derivative at $t=a$ is the usual column vector containing the partial derivatives of the components of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{n}}{∂t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- For functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2)}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2,f_3)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2,x_3)}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199507 1064 "Recall that the quadratic Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the concept of Taylor polynomials to multivariable functions. \n\nGiven a twice-differentiable function $f(x,y),$ the **quadratic Taylor polynomial** of $f$ about the point $(a,b)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx \\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about the origin $O$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater in the lesson, we will develop some intuition for why the quadratic part looks the way it does. But before we do that, let's get some practice working with this formula." 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199507 1064 "Recall that the quadratic Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$\n\nWe can extend the concept of Taylor polynomials to multivariable functions.\n\nGiven a twice-differentiable function $f(x,y)$, the **quadratic Taylor polynomial** of $f$ about the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx f(a, b) + f_{x}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a) + f_{y}(a, b) \\cdot (y - b)^(⏞)^(Linear approximation); \\approx + \\frac{1}{2}(f_{xx}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a)^{2} + 2f_{xy}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a)(y - b) + f_{yy}(a, b) \\cdot (y - b)^{2})_(⏟)_(Quadratic part)\n$$\n\nIn particular, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about the origin $O$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx f(0, 0) + f_{x}(0, 0) \\cdot x + f_{y}(0, 0) \\cdot y; \\approx + \\frac{1}{2}(f_{xx}(0, 0) \\cdot x^{2} + 2f_{xy}(0, 0) \\cdot xy + f_{yy}(0, 0) \\cdot y^{2})\n$$\n\nLater in the lesson, we will develop some intuition for why the quadratic part looks the way it does. But before we do that, let's get some practice working with this formula." 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199508 1064 "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial.\n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$. This can be achieved using matrix notation.\n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx f(a, b) + f_{x}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a) + f_{y}(a, b) \\cdot (y - b)^(⏞)^(Linear approximation); \\approx + \\frac{1}{2}(f_{xx}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a)^{2} + 2f_{xy}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a)(y - b) + f_{yy}(a, b) \\cdot (y - b)^{2})_(⏟)_(Quadratic part)\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ then the total derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nat\n\n$$\n(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f_{x}(a, b) & f_{y}(a, b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLinear approximation &= f(a, b) + f_{x}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a) + f_{y}(a, b) \\cdot (y - b) \\\\\n&= f(a) + \\begin{bmatrix}f_{x}(a, b) & f_{y}(a, b)\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x - a \\\\ y - b\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= f(a) + \\begin{bmatrix}f_{x}(a, b) & f_{y}(a, b)\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f$.\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f_{xx}(a) & f_{xy}(a) \\\\ f_{yx}(a) & f_{yy}(a)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\nf_{xy} = f_{yx}\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQuadratic part &= \\frac{1}{2}(f_{xx}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a)^{2} + 2f_{xy}(a, b) \\cdot (x - a)(y - b) + f_{yy}(a, b) \\cdot (y - b)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{