\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)(x-a)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this formula is more compact and has clear similarity with the corresponding formula for functions with one variable:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 true] [17592186199509 1064 "The formula derived earlier works for multivariable functions with a finite number of variables.\n\nSuppose that the function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand is twice differentiable at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nThen, the quadratic Taylor approximation of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\mid \\approx f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - a)^{T}f^{″}(a)(x - a)\n$$\n\nwhere the first derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{1}∂x_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{2}∂x_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533441 17592186045585 false] [17592186199509 1064 "The formula derived earlier works for multivariable functions with a finite number of variables. \n\nSuppose that the function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor approximation of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & \\approx f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{'